const e=[{id:"sv-5-061",phase:5,topic:"5.1.1",qtype:"table",difficulty:2,stem:`A contact-centre floor is audited at the end of each month. Each S is scored out of **10** (maximum 50) and the control-plan target is **80%**.

| S | Jan | Feb | Mar |
|---|---|---|---|
| Sort | 9 | 9 | 9 |
| Set in order | 9 | 8 | 8 |
| Shine | 8 | 8 | 7 |
| Standardize | 8 | 7 | 6 |
| Sustain | 9 | 7 | 5 |

Which conclusion is correct?`,options:["March scores 35% and is below target; Sustain has fallen the most","March scores 70% and is below target; Sort has fallen the most","March scores 70% and is below target; Sustain has fallen the most","The three-month average is 78%, so the area is effectively on target and no action is needed"],answer:2,hint:"Audit % = total score divided by the maximum possible; then compare each S across months to find the biggest drop.",steps:[{title:"Write what is given",body:"Five S’s × 10 points = 50 maximum. March scores: 9, 8, 7, 6, 5. Target 80%."},{title:"Calculate March",body:"$$\\text{Score}_{Mar}=\\dfrac{9+8+7+6+5}{50}\\times 100\\%=\\dfrac{35}{50}\\times 100\\%=70\\%$$"},{title:"Find the trend",body:"Jan → Mar: Jan $=\\dfrac{43}{50}=86\\%$, Feb $=\\dfrac{39}{50}=78\\%$, Mar 70%. Sort is flat (9, 9, 9); **Sustain** drops 9 → 7 → 5, the largest fall."},{title:"Decide",body:"March is below the 80% target and the decline is led by Sustain (and Standardize). The reaction is to restore the audit routine, ownership and standards — not to repeat Sort."}],final:"March = 70%, Sustain fell most → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Divided 35 by 100 instead of by the 50-point maximum.","Sort is the only S that did not fall (9, 9, 9).","","Averaging three months hides a steady decline; March alone is 10 points below target and falling."],trap:"Read the trend, not just the latest total: a steadily falling Sustain score is the early warning that 5S is slipping.",bookRef:"Book pp. 503–508"},{id:"sv-5-062",phase:5,topic:"5.1.1",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:1,stem:"A data-centre manager wants anyone walking past a server cage to see **within a few seconds** whether it is in its standard condition. Which control BEST achieves this?",options:["A monthly written report of the 5S audit score, emailed to the site manager","CCTV cameras pointed at the cage so the NOC can review footage if problems occur","A longer, more detailed cage-maintenance SOP stored on the intranet","Visual controls: floor markings, labelled cable trays, a shadow board for tools and a photo of the standard condition posted at the cage"],answer:3,hint:"Standardize uses visual controls so that “normal vs abnormal” is obvious at a glance.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book links Standardize to **visual controls** — signboards, painted lines, colour coding — and says 5S is necessary for visual management (empty bins, red/yellow/green signals)."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"“See within seconds” is the definition of a visual control: any deviation from the posted standard (a missing tool, a cable out of its tray) is obvious on the spot."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Reports and SOPs are not visible at the point of work; CCTV records problems after the fact and needs someone to review it."}],final:"Visual controls at the cage → answer **D**",whyWrong:["A monthly report arrives weeks later and is not visible at the cage.","CCTV is after-the-fact review, not an at-a-glance standard.","A longer SOP does not make abnormal conditions visible at a glance.",""],bookRef:"Book pp. 507, 570"},{id:"sv-5-063",phase:5,topic:"5.1.1",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:2,stem:"A field-operations depot ran a successful 5S programme. The continuous-improvement (CI) team did every monthly audit. When the CI team moved to a new project, audits stopped and scores fell from 88% to 61% in four months. What is the BEST fix?",options:["Make the depot’s own supervisors and manager own the audits on a fixed schedule, with results reviewed in their routine meetings and actions tracked","Raise the audit target from 80% to 90% so that the depot tries harder","Ask the CI team to come back and run another 5S event","Replace 5S audits with an annual external inspection"],answer:0,hint:"Sustain depends on the process owner — not the project team — owning the audit loop.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book stresses that 5S strength depends on the commitment to maintain it, and that the last two S’s are sustaining actions. Controls must survive the Belt or CI team leaving."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"Scores fell exactly when the audit owner left. The audit routine must be handed to the people who run the depot, built into their schedule and management review."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Another event restores the area briefly; a higher target with no owner changes nothing; an annual inspection is far too infrequent to sustain daily habits."}],final:"Transfer audit ownership to the process owner → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","A higher target is meaningless when nobody is auditing.","A repeat event restores the area but still depends on the CI team — the same failure will recur.","An annual inspection cannot sustain daily discipline; gaps would go unseen for months."],bookRef:"Book pp. 503–508"},{id:"sv-5-064",phase:5,topic:"5.1.1",qtype:"concept",difficulty:1,stem:"The book places 5S at the **base** of the Lean tools pyramid, below the visual factory, standardized work, kaizen and kanban. What does this position mean?",options:["The higher tools cannot be sustained without an organised, standardised workplace, so 5S must be in place first","5S should only be used after kanban has been implemented","5S is the least important Lean tool and can be skipped if time is short","5S is used only in the Define phase"],answer:0,hint:"Pyramid = building blocks: each level rests on the one below it.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book’s pyramid runs 5S → visual factory → standardized work → kaizen → kanban, and it warns that kaizens and kanbans cannot be attempted without organised workplaces and work instructions."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"The base is the foundation, not the least important level; the order runs 5S first, not kanban first; 5S is used across phases and reviewed again in Control."}],final:"Foundation for the other Lean tools → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","This reverses the pyramid: kanban sits at the top and needs 5S underneath it.","The base is the foundation that everything else rests on — skipping it undermines the higher tools.","The book uses Lean tools at any time and revisits 5S in Control."],bookRef:"Book pp. 502, 513"},{id:"sv-5-065",phase:5,topic:"5.1.1",qtype:"not-except",difficulty:1,stem:"All of the following are controls that help **sustain** 5S in a spare-parts store EXCEPT:",options:["A shadow board showing the outline of each tool","Minimum and maximum lines painted on the shelves next to each part","A red/yellow/green board showing this week’s audit result for each zone","A one-off deep clean of the store by an outside contractor"],answer:3,hint:"Sustaining controls are routine, visual and owned; a one-off event is not a control.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Sustaining needs standards that are visible and checked repeatedly — visual controls, schedules and checklists (book pp. 507–508)."},{title:"Apply",body:"Shadow boards, min/max lines and a traffic-light audit board are all permanent visual controls. A one-off deep clean is a Shine action done once, by outsiders, with no owner or routine."}],final:"One-off contractor clean is NOT a sustaining control → answer **D**",whyWrong:["A shadow board is a classic visual control — a missing tool is obvious at once.","Min/max lines make the stock standard visible at the point of use.","A traffic-light board shows audit status and triggers attention to red zones.",""],bookRef:"Book pp. 503–508"},{id:"sv-5-066",phase:5,topic:"5.1.1",qtype:"true-false",difficulty:1,stem:"If a newly organised area scores 100% on its first 5S audit, it can be taken off the audit schedule because the standard has been achieved. True or False?",options:["True","False"],answer:1,hint:"Sustain = continuous assessment; one good score is not proof that the standard will hold.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book describes Standardize as maintained by **continuous assessment**, and Sustain as kept by schedules and checklists."},{title:"Apply",body:"The first audit right after the event is the easiest one to pass. Removing the audit removes the feedback loop that keeps the area from slipping back."}],final:"False → answer **B**",whyWrong:["A single high score straight after the event says nothing about whether the habits will last; audits must continue.",""],bookRef:"Book pp. 503–508"},{id:"sv-5-067",phase:5,topic:"5.1.2",qtype:"calculation",difficulty:1,stem:"A retail store sells **45 prepaid SIM packs per day**. Replenishment from the warehouse takes **4 days**, the safety factor is **20%**, and each box holds **20 packs**. How many kanban cards should the loop have?",options:["9","10","11","216"],answer:2,hint:"$$N=\\dfrac{D\\times L\\times(1+S)}{C}$$ — round up to a whole card.",steps:[{title:"Write what is given",body:"D = 45 packs per day, L = 4 days, S = 0.20, C = 20 packs per box."},{title:"Pick the formula",body:"$$N=\\dfrac{D\\times L\\times(1+S)}{C}$$"},{title:"Substitute and calculate",body:"$$N=\\dfrac{45\\times 4\\times 1.2}{20}=\\dfrac{216}{20}=10.8$$"},{title:"Decide",body:"Round **up** to 11 cards so the loop never runs short."}],final:"N = 10.8 → 11 cards → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Left out the safety factor: 45 × 4 ÷ 20 = 9.","Rounded 10.8 down; kanban counts are always rounded up.","","Forgot to divide by the box size — 216 is packs, not cards."],bookRef:"Book pp. 511–512"},{id:"sv-5-068",phase:5,topic:"5.1.2",qtype:"calculation",difficulty:3,stem:"A kanban loop for fibre splice trays has **12 cards**, each for a bin of **25 trays**. Demand is **100 trays per day** and replenishment takes **2.5 days**. What safety factor is built into this loop?",options:["16.7%","20%","83.3%","120%"],answer:1,hint:"Rearrange $$N=\\dfrac{D\\times L\\times(1+S)}{C}$$ to solve for S.",steps:[{title:"Write what is given",body:"N = 12 cards, C = 25 trays, D = 100 trays per day, L = 2.5 days."},{title:"Rearrange the formula",body:"$$1+S=\\dfrac{N\\times C}{D\\times L}$$"},{title:"Substitute and calculate",body:"$$1+S=\\dfrac{12\\times 25}{100\\times 2.5}=\\dfrac{300}{250}=1.20$$"},{title:"Decide",body:"S = 1.20 − 1 = 0.20, so the loop carries a **20%** safety allowance above lead-time demand."}],final:"S = 20% → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Divided the 50-tray excess by the loop stock (300) instead of by lead-time demand (250).","","Reported lead-time demand ÷ loop stock (250 ÷ 300), which is not the safety factor.","Reported (1 + S) = 1.20 as the safety factor instead of subtracting 1."],bookRef:"Book pp. 511–512"},{id:"sv-5-069",phase:5,topic:"5.1.2",qtype:"calculation",difficulty:2,stem:"A depot replenishes ONT units with D = **160 per day**, safety factor **10%** and **40 units per container**. A new courier contract cuts lead time from **4 days to 2.5 days**. How many kanban cards can be **removed** from the loop?",options:["6","7","11","18"],answer:1,hint:"Compute $$N=\\dfrac{D\\times L\\times(1+S)}{C}$$ before and after, rounding each UP, then subtract.",steps:[{title:"Write what is given",body:"D = 160, S = 0.10, C = 40; L falls from 4 to 2.5 days."},{title:"Cards before",body:"$$N_{before}=\\dfrac{160\\times 4\\times 1.1}{40}=\\dfrac{704}{40}=17.6\\rightarrow 18$$"},{title:"Cards after",body:"$$N_{after}=\\dfrac{160\\times 2.5\\times 1.1}{40}=\\dfrac{440}{40}=11$$"},{title:"Decide",body:"18 − 11 = **7 cards** can be withdrawn, cutting loop inventory by 7 × 40 = 280 units with the same protection."}],final:"18 − 11 = 7 cards removed → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Subtracted the unrounded counts (17.6 − 11 = 6.6) and rounded down; each loop size must be rounded up first.","","This is the new number of cards, not the number removed.","This is the old number of cards, not the number removed."],bookRef:"Book pp. 511–512"},{id:"sv-5-070",phase:5,topic:"5.1.2",qtype:"table",difficulty:2,stem:`A depot audits four kanban loops. All use a safety factor of **10%**.

| Part | Demand per day | Lead time (days) | Container size | Cards in loop |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| ONT units | 60 | 2 | 20 | 8 |
| Optical splitters | 30 | 3 | 10 | 10 |
| Patch cords | 200 | 1 | 50 | 9 |
| Backup batteries | 12 | 5 | 6 | 11 |

Which loop holds the **most excess cards** (and therefore excess inventory)?`,options:["ONT units","Optical splitters","Patch cords","Backup batteries"],answer:2,hint:"Required cards $$N=\\dfrac{D\\times L\\times(1+S)}{C}$$ rounded up; excess = cards in loop − required.",steps:[{title:"Pick the formula",body:"$$N=\\dfrac{D\\times L\\times(1+S)}{C}$$"},{title:"Required cards",body:`$$N_{ONT}=\\dfrac{60\\times 2\\times 1.1}{20}=6.6\\rightarrow 7$$

$$N_{split}=\\dfrac{30\\times 3\\times 1.1}{10}=9.9\\rightarrow 10$$

$$N_{patch}=\\dfrac{200\\times 1\\times 1.1}{50}=4.4\\rightarrow 5$$

$$N_{batt}=\\dfrac{12\\times 5\\times 1.1}{6}=11$$`},{title:"Compare with the loop",body:"Excess cards: ONT 8 − 7 = 1; splitters 10 − 10 = 0; patch cords 9 − 5 = **4**; batteries 11 − 11 = 0."},{title:"Decide",body:"The patch-cord loop carries 4 extra containers (200 cords) beyond what demand, lead time and safety need."}],final:"Patch cords: 9 − 5 = 4 excess cards → answer **C**",whyWrong:["ONT has only 1 excess card (8 vs 7).","Splitters need 9.9 → 10 cards; the loop is right-sized.","","Batteries need exactly 11 cards — the largest card count, but not excess."],trap:"The loop with the most cards is not necessarily the oversized one — compare with the requirement.",bookRef:"Book pp. 511–512"},{id:"sv-5-071",phase:5,topic:"5.1.2",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:2,stem:"A spare-parts kanban loop was sized correctly, yet stock-outs keep happening. A walk-through finds several kanban cards in engineers’ pockets and van glove boxes days after the containers were emptied. What is the BEST response?",options:["Set a card-handling standard (return the card to the post the moment a container is opened or emptied) and audit the number of cards in circulation regularly","Switch back to ordering against the monthly forecast","Add more cards to the loop to cover the missing ones","Increase the safety factor from 10% to 50%"],answer:0,hint:"A kanban only works if the signal travels immediately; fix card discipline before changing the card count.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"A kanban is a **signal** to replenish (book p. 511). A card that is not returned means the signal is never sent, so the loop behaves as if it had fewer cards."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"The loop size is right; the problem is the delay in returning cards. A standard for card handling plus a regular card-count audit restores the signal."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Adding cards or raising the safety factor hides the discipline problem with extra inventory; returning to forecast ordering abandons pull altogether."}],final:"Card-handling standard plus card audit → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Abandoning pull for push removes the benefits of kanban instead of fixing the signal.","Extra cards mask the root cause and inflate inventory; lost cards will keep disappearing.","A bigger safety factor adds inventory to cover a discipline problem."],bookRef:"Book pp. 511–512"},{id:"sv-5-072",phase:5,topic:"5.1.2",qtype:"concept",difficulty:1,stem:"In the kanban formula, what does the **safety factor** protect against?",options:["Variation in demand and in replenishment lead time during the loop","Defective parts arriving from the supplier","The cost of printing and replacing cards","Differences between container sizes"],answer:0,hint:"$$N=\\dfrac{D\\times L\\times(1+S)}{C}$$ — S adds a buffer on top of average lead-time demand.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"D × L is the **average** demand during the replenishment time. Real demand and lead time vary, so S adds a buffer so that the loop does not run out when demand is high or delivery is slow."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Supplier quality is handled by inspection or supplier controls, not by S; card cost and container size are separate design choices (C is already in the formula)."}],final:"Variation in demand and lead time → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Supplier defects are a quality issue; inflating stock to cover them hides the problem.","Card cost has nothing to do with the number of units needed.","Container size is the separate C term in the formula."],bookRef:"Book pp. 511–512"},{id:"sv-5-073",phase:5,topic:"5.1.2",qtype:"true-false",difficulty:1,stem:"Kanban is a push system: cards are issued according to the production or purchasing schedule. True or False?",options:["True","False"],answer:1,hint:"Kanban = pull: work or material is triggered by actual consumption downstream.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book defines kanban as the technique used to **pull** products and material through the system; the signal is typically an empty container or a card."},{title:"Apply",body:"In a push system, work is released to a schedule or forecast. A kanban card is released only when downstream use creates the need, so the statement is false."}],final:"False — kanban is pull → answer **B**",whyWrong:["This describes push (schedule-driven) replenishment; kanban is triggered by consumption.",""],bookRef:"Book p. 511"},{id:"sv-5-074",phase:5,topic:"5.1.3",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:2,stem:"Billing agents type promotional discount codes into a free-text field. About 3% of codes are mistyped or not valid for the customer’s plan, and customers are overcharged until they complain. Which poka-yoke is BEST?",options:["Run a weekly report listing invalid codes so that billing can issue corrections","Replace the free-text field with a drop-down that lists only the codes valid for that customer’s plan","Retrain agents on the code list every quarter","Ask a supervisor to check a 10% sample of discounts each day"],answer:1,hint:"Prevention poka-yoke makes the error impossible at the point it would occur.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The strongest control in the book is a **Type 1 countermeasure** — a mistake-proofing or design change that stops the error condition from happening at all."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"A drop-down restricted to valid codes means an invalid or mistyped code cannot be entered. The defect is never created."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"A weekly report and a 10% sample are after-the-fact inspection; retraining depends on memory and is the weakest form of control."}],final:"Drop-down of valid codes (prevention) → answer **B**",whyWrong:["A weekly report detects errors after customers are already overcharged.","","Training relies on people remembering — the book ranks this lowest in effectiveness.","Sampling inspection lets 90% of errors through unchecked."],bookRef:"Book p. 522"},{id:"sv-5-075",phase:5,topic:"5.1.3",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:3,stem:"During network changes, engineers occasionally run the **rollback script before taking the configuration backup**, which leaves no restore point. Which poka-yoke design addresses this BEST?",options:["Make the change tool lock the rollback step until a backup file with a valid checksum is recorded for the device","Review all completed changes the next morning to find any without a backup","Add a bold warning to the change procedure: “Always back up first”","Ask a second engineer to watch every change"],answer:0,hint:"Sequence (motion-step) poka-yoke: the next step is physically or logically blocked until the earlier step is done.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"A **sequence** poka-yoke enforces the order of steps. A control-type (shutdown) device stops the process instead of only warning."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"Locking the rollback step until a valid backup exists makes the wrong order impossible — a Type 1 prevention."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"A warning still relies on attention; a next-morning review detects the error after the damage; a second engineer is costly inspection that can also fail."}],final:"Lock the step until the backup exists → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Next-morning review is inspection after the failure has already happened.","A warning in the procedure is an SOP control — the weakest in the book’s ranking.","A second watcher is human inspection: costly, and it can miss the same error."],bookRef:"Book p. 522"},{id:"sv-5-076",phase:5,topic:"5.1.3",qtype:"concept",difficulty:2,stem:"The book ranks control methods by effectiveness. Which control ranks **second**, just below a Type 1 countermeasure?",options:["SPC on the X’s with trained staff who are empowered to stop the process","A standard operating procedure (SOP)","A Type 3 containment such as 100% inspection","A Type 2 flag: a detection poka-yoke that spots the error condition and stops the process so the defect cannot move forward"],answer:3,hint:"Order: Type 1 (prevent) → Type 2 (detect and stop) → SPC with empowered staff → Type 3 inspection → SPC without empowerment → SOP.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Book p. 522: the most effective control is a Type 1 corrective action (prevention poka-yoke). The **second** most effective is a Type 2 corrective action — a flag that detects the error condition and shuts the process down."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"SPC with empowered staff ranks third; Type 3 inspection is short-term containment; an SOP alone is described as not sustainable."}],final:"Type 2 flag (detection poka-yoke that stops the process) → answer **D**",whyWrong:["SPC with empowered staff ranks third, below the Type 2 flag.","An SOP is the lowest-ranked method, “not sustainable short-term or long-term”.","Inspection is Type 3 — short-term containment.",""],bookRef:"Book p. 522"},{id:"sv-5-077",phase:5,topic:"5.1.3",qtype:"not-except",difficulty:1,stem:"Which of the following is NOT a feature of a good poka-yoke?",options:["It is simple and usually inexpensive","It gives immediate feedback at the point where the error happens","It relies on the operator remembering to carry out an extra check","It is placed as close as possible to the source of the error"],answer:2,hint:"Poka-yoke removes the need for memory and attention.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Mistake proofing is designed so that people cannot make the error, or the error is caught instantly — without depending on memory or vigilance."},{title:"Apply",body:"Simple, cheap, immediate and at the source are all hallmarks. “Relies on the operator remembering” is the opposite: that is an SOP, not a poka-yoke."}],final:"Relying on memory is NOT poka-yoke → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Simplicity and low cost are typical poka-yoke features.","Immediate feedback at the point of error is a core feature.","","Placing the device at the source stops the error before it travels."],bookRef:"Book p. 522"},{id:"sv-5-078",phase:5,topic:"5.1.3",qtype:"table",difficulty:3,stem:`An FMEA row for fibre activation reads:

| Failure mode | S | O | D | RPN |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| Wrong VLAN assigned to customer | 7 | 5 | 8 | 280 |

A **prevention** poka-yoke is added: the provisioning system now assigns the VLAN automatically from the port record, so the error is expected to occur only rarely (new rating 2). No new detection step is added. What is the revised row?`,options:["S 7, O 5, D 2 → RPN 70","S 2, O 5, D 8 → RPN 80","S 7, O 2, D 8 → RPN 112","S 2, O 2, D 2 → RPN 8"],answer:2,hint:"RPN = S × O × D. Prevention lowers Occurrence; detection devices lower Detection; Severity rarely changes.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"A prevention poka-yoke stops the cause from producing the failure, so it lowers **Occurrence**. Detection is unchanged because nothing new detects the error; Severity is unchanged because the effect on the customer is the same if it does happen."},{title:"Calculate",body:"$$RPN=S\\times O\\times D=7\\times 2\\times 8=112$$"},{title:"Decide",body:"RPN falls from 280 to 112, driven by the occurrence rating."}],final:"S 7, O 2, D 8 → RPN 112 → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Treated a prevention device as a detection device (lowered D instead of O).","Lowered severity — the effect of a wrong VLAN on the customer does not change.","","Lowered all three ratings; only the occurrence rating is affected by this change."],bookRef:"Book pp. 133, 522"},{id:"sv-5-079",phase:5,topic:"5.1.3",qtype:"true-false",difficulty:1,stem:"A poka-yoke does not have to be a physical device — a software validation rule that blocks an invalid entry is also mistake proofing. True or False?",options:["True","False"],answer:0,hint:"Poka-yoke = any mechanism that prevents or immediately detects an error, physical or digital.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Poka-yoke means “mistake-proof” (book glossary p. 587). The principle is to design the error out; the mechanism can be a fixture, a connector shape or a system rule."},{title:"Apply",body:"In service and telecom processes most poka-yokes are digital: mandatory fields, format checks, drop-downs and workflow locks."}],final:"True → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Software rules are among the most common poka-yokes in service processes."],bookRef:"Book pp. 522, 587"},{id:"sv-5-080",phase:5,topic:"5.2.1",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:1,stem:"Every night a backup job runs on the **same fleet of 400 servers**. Each server’s backup either succeeds or fails. The team wants to chart the **number of failed backups per night**. Which control chart is MOST appropriate?",options:["c chart","u chart","I-MR chart","np chart"],answer:3,hint:"Defectives (pass/fail units) with a constant subgroup size → np; varying size → p.",steps:[{title:"Classify the data",body:"Each server is a unit that is either defective (failed) or not — this is **defectives** (binomial) data, and the subgroup size is constant at 400."},{title:"Pick the chart",body:"Counts of defective units with a constant n → **np chart** (book selection tree pp. 530–531)."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"c and u charts are for **defects** (counts of flaws, several possible per unit); an I-MR chart is for continuous individual values."}],final:"Defectives, constant n = 400 → np chart → answer **D**",whyWrong:["A c chart counts defects (flaws) in a constant area of opportunity, not defective units.","A u chart is for defects per unit with a varying area of opportunity.","I-MR is for continuous measurements taken one at a time, not pass/fail counts.",""],bookRef:"Book pp. 530–531"},{id:"sv-5-081",phase:5,topic:"5.2.1",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:1,stem:"A mobile operator tracks **customer complaints per 1,000 active subscribers** each week. The subscriber base grows from 410,000 to 520,000 over the year, and a subscriber may complain more than once. Which control chart is MOST appropriate?",options:["u chart","c chart","p chart","np chart"],answer:0,hint:"Defects (several possible per unit) with a varying area of opportunity → u chart.",steps:[{title:"Classify the data",body:"Complaints are **defects** — one subscriber can raise several, so this is a count of events, not a pass/fail classification."},{title:"Check the area of opportunity",body:"The number of subscribers (the area of opportunity) changes every week, so the count must be scaled to a rate per unit."},{title:"Pick the chart",body:"Defects per unit with a varying area of opportunity → **u chart**, whose limits vary with each week’s size."}],final:"Defects per unit, varying base → u chart → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","A c chart needs a constant area of opportunity; the subscriber base is growing.","A p chart is for defectives (each unit pass/fail); a subscriber can complain more than once.","An np chart is for defective counts with a constant sample size."],bookRef:"Book pp. 530–531"},{id:"sv-5-082",phase:5,topic:"5.2.1",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:2,stem:"Field-visit dispatch time is measured at **three dispatch centres** that use different tools. An analyst forms each hourly subgroup from **one job from each centre** (n = 3). The Xbar chart shows very wide limits and almost no signals. What should the analyst do?",options:["Keep the plan, because mixing centres captures all the variation in the business","Increase the subgroup to one job from each centre every 30 minutes","Switch to an np chart so that the centres can be combined","Form subgroups from consecutive jobs within one centre and keep a separate chart for each centre"],answer:3,hint:"Rational subgroup = units made under nearly identical conditions; separate streams get separate charts.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book defines a rational subgroup as items produced under nearly identical conditions, and says five items from different machines are **not** rational. It also advises separate charts for separate process streams (pp. 517–519)."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"Mixing centres puts centre-to-centre differences inside each subgroup. The within-subgroup range is inflated, so the limits are too wide and real shifts are hidden."},{title:"Decide",body:"Subgroup consecutive jobs **within** a centre and chart each centre separately."}],final:"Subgroup within a centre; one chart per centre → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Mixing streams inflates within-subgroup variation and makes the limits too wide to detect shifts.","More frequent subgroups still mix the three centres in every subgroup.","Dispatch time is continuous data; an np chart is for counts of defectives.",""],bookRef:"Book pp. 517–519"},{id:"sv-5-083",phase:5,topic:"5.2.1",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:3,stem:"A NOC records core-link latency **every second** and plots the values on an I-MR chart. Consecutive readings are strongly correlated (one high second is usually followed by another), and the chart signals many times a day with no special cause found. What is the BEST change?",options:["Widen the limits to ±4σ so that fewer signals appear","Sample less often (for example, one reading every few minutes) or use an EWMA chart that is designed to handle autocorrelated data","Switch the chart to a p chart of the percentage of high-latency seconds","Respond to every signal by restarting the router"],answer:1,hint:"Autocorrelated data break the independence assumption behind Shewhart limits; space out the samples or use EWMA.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Shewhart limits assume independent points. With autocorrelation, MR̄ is small (neighbours are similar), so the limits are too narrow and false alarms multiply. The book lists EWMA for autocorrelated output (p. 531)."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"Sampling less often reduces the correlation between points; an EWMA chart is the book’s special-case chart for this situation."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Wider limits hide real signals as well; turning continuous data into a p chart throws information away; restarting on false alarms is tampering."}],final:"Space out the samples or use EWMA → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Arbitrarily widening limits lowers sensitivity to real special causes and does not fix the cause of the false alarms.","","Converting continuous latency into pass/fail data loses information and does not remove the autocorrelation.","Reacting to false alarms is tampering and adds variation."],bookRef:"Book pp. 529–531"},{id:"sv-5-084",phase:5,topic:"5.2.1",qtype:"output",difficulty:2,stem:`A Phase II Xbar-R chart is set up as follows:

\`\`\`
Xbar-R Chart of Provisioning Time (h)
Subgroup size = 5
Estimated historical parameters used:
  Mean   = 30.00   (from Phase I, subgroups 1-25)
  R-bar  = 4.65    (from Phase I, subgroups 1-25)

Xbar Chart:  UCL = 32.68   CL = 30.00   LCL = 27.32
Points plotted: 26-40 (new data)
Test Results: none
\`\`\`

What is the effect of entering the historical parameters?`,options:["Minitab recalculates the limits from all 40 subgroups each time new data are added","The new subgroups are judged against the fixed Phase I limits, which change only after a verified process change","The limits are set equal to the customer specification limits","The limits automatically narrow as more data are added, making the chart more sensitive"],answer:1,hint:"Phase II: freeze the Phase I parameters so new points are judged against them.",steps:[{title:"Locate",body:"The output says “Estimated historical parameters used” — the mean and R̄ come from Phase I."},{title:"Check the limits",body:"$$UCL=\\bar{\\bar{x}}+A_2\\bar{R}=30.00+0.577\\times 4.65=32.68$$ — computed from the frozen Phase I values only."},{title:"Decide",body:"Points 26–40 are judged against fixed limits. The book advises entering historical parameters so that new points do not shift the limits (p. 547), and recalculating only after a verified change or at a periodic review (p. 553)."}],final:"Fixed Phase I limits for new data → answer **B**",whyWrong:["That is what happens WITHOUT historical parameters — the limits creep and can hide a drift.","","Control limits never come from the specification; they are the voice of the process.","Frozen limits do not change as data are added."],bookRef:"Book pp. 547, 553"},{id:"sv-5-085",phase:5,topic:"5.2.1",qtype:"output",difficulty:2,stem:`A Green Belt has just built a new chart:

\`\`\`
Xbar-R Chart of Ticket Triage Time (min)
Number of subgroups = 8      Subgroup size = 5

Xbar Chart:  UCL = 18.42   CL = 15.10   LCL = 11.78
R Chart:     UCL = 12.17   CL = 5.756   LCL = 0
No test failures.
\`\`\`

What is the MAIN concern before these limits are used to monitor the process?`,options:["Only 8 subgroups were used; trial limits should be based on at least 20–25 subgroups","The R-chart LCL of 0 shows that the calculation is wrong","The subgroup size of 5 is too small for an Xbar-R chart","No test failures means the process is capable, so a capability study is not needed"],answer:0,hint:"Phase I needs about 20–25 subgroups before limits are trusted.",steps:[{title:"Locate",body:"“Number of subgroups = 8”."},{title:"Apply the rule",body:"The book asks for at least 20 samples before control limits are calculated (p. 521), and Phase I commonly uses 20–25 rational subgroups. With 8 subgroups, R̄ and $\\bar{\\bar{x}}$ are poorly estimated, so the limits may be far from the true ones."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"n = 5 is a typical Xbar-R subgroup size; D₃ = 0 for n ≤ 6, so an R-chart LCL of 0 is correct; stability does not prove capability."}],final:"Too few subgroups (8) for trial limits → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","For n ≤ 6, D₃ = 0, so the R-chart LCL is correctly 0.","Subgroups of 3–5 are the most common choice for Xbar-R charts.","In control (stable) is not the same as capable; capability compares the process with the specification."],bookRef:"Book pp. 521, 528"},{id:"sv-5-086",phase:5,topic:"5.2.1",qtype:"concept",difficulty:1,stem:"Which statement BEST describes a **rational subgroup**?",options:["A random sample drawn from the whole month’s output","The largest subgroup that the sampling budget allows","A group that contains one unit from every machine, shift and operator","A group of units produced under nearly identical conditions, so that variation within the subgroup is common-cause only"],answer:3,hint:"Within-subgroup = common cause only; between-subgroup = where special causes show up.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book defines a rational subgroup as items made under nearly identical conditions — for example, consecutive units from one machine (pp. 517–519). Within-subgroup variation then estimates common-cause noise, and the limits can detect shifts between subgroups."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Spreading a subgroup across the month, or across machines and shifts, puts special causes inside the subgroup and inflates the limits; size is a separate decision."}],final:"Units made under nearly identical conditions → answer **D**",whyWrong:["A month-wide random sample mixes many conditions inside one subgroup.","Subgroup size is a separate choice; a large subgroup can still be non-rational.","One unit from every machine, shift and operator mixes sources — the book’s example of a non-rational subgroup.",""],bookRef:"Book pp. 517–519"},{id:"sv-5-087",phase:5,topic:"5.2.1",qtype:"concept",difficulty:2,stem:"Why does an Xbar chart with **larger subgroups** detect a shift in the process mean more readily than one with smaller subgroups?",options:["Larger subgroups make the process itself less variable","The standard error of the subgroup mean falls as n grows, so the Xbar limits are narrower around the centre line","Larger subgroups use wider control limits, which catch more points","Larger subgroups remove the need for the R or S chart"],answer:1,hint:"$$\\sigma_{\\bar{x}}=\\dfrac{\\sigma}{\\sqrt{n}}$$ — limits sit at ±3 standard errors.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The Xbar limits are $\\bar{\\bar{x}}\\pm 3\\sigma_{\\bar{x}}$ with $$\\sigma_{\\bar{x}}=\\dfrac{\\sigma}{\\sqrt{n}}$$ so the limits tighten as n grows."},{title:"Apply",body:"A fixed shift in the mean is a larger number of standard errors when n is bigger, so a point beyond the limits is more likely. Sampling cost and the need for rational subgroups set the practical size (book: 3–12, most often 3, 5 or 10)."}],final:"Smaller standard error → narrower Xbar limits → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Sampling more units does not change the process variation itself.","","Limits get narrower, not wider, as n increases.","The spread chart is still needed to check within-subgroup variation."],bookRef:"Book p. 528"},{id:"sv-5-088",phase:5,topic:"5.2.1",qtype:"not-except",difficulty:2,stem:"All of the following are good practices when collecting data for a new control chart EXCEPT:",options:["Collect the data while the process is operating normally","Record notes of events (staff changes, system releases) alongside the data","Sample more frequently at first, until the process behaviour is understood","Combine units from different teams in each subgroup so that every subgroup covers all sources of variation"],answer:3,hint:"Rational subgroups keep different sources apart; mixing sources inflates the limits.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book’s data-collection advice: unbiased sampling, process operating normally, sample more at first, keep separate charts for separate streams, and form rational subgroups (pp. 517–519)."},{title:"Apply",body:"Combining teams in each subgroup puts team-to-team differences inside the subgroup — the classic error that makes limits too wide."}],final:"Mixing teams in a subgroup is NOT good practice → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Sampling a normally operating process is required for a valid baseline.","Event notes are essential for finding special causes later.","Sampling more at first is the book’s own advice.",""],bookRef:"Book pp. 517–519"},{id:"sv-5-089",phase:5,topic:"5.2.1",qtype:"sequence",difficulty:1,stem:`A team is starting SPC on order-fallout rate. Put these steps in the correct order:

- **P** — Choose the characteristic to chart and the chart type
- **Q** — Confirm the measurement system is adequate
- **R** — Collect 20–25 rational subgroups
- **S** — Calculate trial limits and investigate any signals`,options:["Q → P → S → R","P → R → Q → S","P → Q → R → S","R → P → Q → S"],answer:2,hint:"Decide what to measure → trust the measurement → collect → calculate.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"You cannot collect useful data until you know what to chart and that the measurement is trustworthy; limits come from the collected data."},{title:"Order",body:"P (what and which chart) → Q (MSA) → R (collect 20–25 subgroups) → S (trial limits, investigate signals)."}],final:"P → Q → R → S → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Limits cannot be calculated before the data are collected.","Data collected before the MSA may have to be discarded if the gauge is inadequate.","","Collecting data before choosing the characteristic and chart risks collecting the wrong data."],bookRef:"Book pp. 517–519"},{id:"sv-5-090",phase:5,topic:"5.2.1",qtype:"table",difficulty:2,stem:`A contact centre wants an Xbar-R chart of **handle time** that detects hour-to-hour shifts. Three sampling plans are proposed:

| Plan | How each subgroup is formed |
|---|---|
| 1 | 5 consecutive calls handled by **one team**, taken every hour |
| 2 | **1 call from each of five teams**, taken every hour |
| 3 | 25 calls drawn at random across the **whole day**, once a day |

Which plan gives the BEST rational subgroups for this purpose?`,options:["Plan 1","Plan 2","Plan 3","Plans 2 and 3 equally"],answer:0,hint:"Rational subgroup: short time window, one stream; frequency matched to the shifts you want to detect.",steps:[{title:"Check each plan",body:"Plan 1: consecutive calls, one team, short window → within-subgroup variation is common-cause only; hourly frequency matches the aim. Plan 2: mixes five teams in every subgroup. Plan 3: one subgroup per day spread over the whole day — hour-to-hour shifts end up inside the subgroup."},{title:"Decide",body:"Plan 1 is rational and frequent enough. (Other teams would get their own charts.)"}],final:"Plan 1 → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Mixing teams inflates within-subgroup variation and hides shifts.","A daily subgroup spread across the whole day absorbs the hourly shifts it is meant to detect.","Both plans mix sources of variation inside the subgroup."],bookRef:"Book pp. 517–519, 528–529"},{id:"sv-5-091",phase:5,topic:"5.2.1",qtype:"true-false",difficulty:1,stem:"When a control chart is applied to a process **output** (Y), the book describes it as statistical process **monitoring**, because the output is controlled by controlling the inputs (X’s). True or False?",options:["True","False"],answer:0,hint:"Y = f(X): monitor the Y; control the X’s.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book notes that SPC on outputs is really statistical process monitoring (SPM): you cannot adjust a Y directly, only the X’s that drive it (p. 516)."},{title:"Apply",body:"That is why control plans put SPC on the critical X’s where possible and use charts on Y’s to confirm the gain holds."}],final:"True → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","The book uses exactly this distinction: SPC on outputs is monitoring; control comes from the inputs."],bookRef:"Book pp. 516, 526"},{id:"sv-5-092",phase:5,topic:"5.2.2",qtype:"calculation",difficulty:2,stem:`Daily **change-approval time** (hours) for 7 consecutive days:

**14.2, 15.0, 13.6, 14.8, 15.4, 14.0, 14.4**

Using an I-MR chart, what is the **LCL of the Individuals chart**?`,options:["11.33","11.59","11.91","12.28"],answer:2,hint:"$$\\overline{MR}=\\dfrac{\\sum |x_i-x_{i-1}|}{k-1}\\qquad LCL=\\bar{x}-2.66\\,\\overline{MR}$$",steps:[{title:"Write what is given",body:"k = 7 values; there are k − 1 = 6 moving ranges."},{title:"Mean",body:"$$\\bar{x}=\\dfrac{101.4}{7}=14.486$$"},{title:"Average moving range",body:"Moving ranges: 0.8, 1.4, 1.2, 0.6, 1.4, 0.4. $$\\overline{MR}=\\dfrac{5.8}{6}=0.967$$"},{title:"Calculate the LCL",body:"$$LCL=\\bar{x}-2.66\\,\\overline{MR}=14.486-2.66\\times 0.967=11.91$$"},{title:"Decide",body:"LCL ≈ **11.91 h** (UCL ≈ 17.06 h)."}],final:"LCL = 11.91 h → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Used D₄ = 3.267 (the MR-chart constant) instead of 2.66.","Used 3 × MR̄, forgetting to divide by d₂ (2.66 = 3 ÷ 1.128).","","Divided the sum of the moving ranges by 7 values instead of by 6 ranges."],bookRef:"Book p. 538"},{id:"sv-5-093",phase:5,topic:"5.2.2",qtype:"calculation",difficulty:2,stem:"An I chart of **daily new-connection orders** (hundreds) shows **UCL = 62.0** and **LCL = 38.0**. What is the estimated process standard deviation $\\hat{\\sigma}$?",options:["2.00","4.00","4.51","8.00"],answer:1,hint:"I-chart limits sit at $\\bar{x}\\pm 3\\hat{\\sigma}$, so the distance between them is $6\\hat{\\sigma}$.",steps:[{title:"Write what is given",body:"UCL = 62.0, LCL = 38.0 (so CL = 50.0)."},{title:"Pick the relationship",body:"$$UCL-LCL=6\\hat{\\sigma}\\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad\\hat{\\sigma}=\\dfrac{UCL-LCL}{6}$$"},{title:"Calculate",body:"$$\\hat{\\sigma}=\\dfrac{62.0-38.0}{6}=\\dfrac{24}{6}=4.00$$"},{title:"Check",body:"Equivalently $$\\overline{MR}=\\dfrac{24}{2\\times 2.66}=4.51\\quad\\text{and}\\quad\\hat{\\sigma}=\\dfrac{4.51}{1.128}=4.00$$"}],final:"σ̂ = 4.00 → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Divided the full width by 12 (treated it as ±6σ).","","This is MR̄ (width ÷ 5.32), not σ̂; σ̂ = MR̄ ÷ 1.128.","Divided the width by 3 instead of by 6 (half-width ÷ 1.5)."],bookRef:"Book p. 538"},{id:"sv-5-094",phase:5,topic:"5.2.2",qtype:"calculation",difficulty:3,stem:`Six consecutive **jitter** readings (ms) on a VoIP trunk, one per hour:

**3.1, 2.6, 3.4, 2.9, 3.3, 2.5**

What is the **UCL of the Moving Range chart**?`,options:["1.60","1.63","1.80","1.96"],answer:3,hint:"$$\\overline{MR}=\\dfrac{\\sum MR}{k-1}\\qquad UCL_{MR}=D_4\\,\\overline{MR}=3.267\\,\\overline{MR}$$",steps:[{title:"Write what is given",body:"k = 6 readings → 5 moving ranges."},{title:"Moving ranges",body:"0.5, 0.8, 0.5, 0.4, 0.8 → $$\\overline{MR}=\\dfrac{3.0}{5}=0.60$$"},{title:"Calculate",body:"$$UCL_{MR}=3.267\\times 0.60=1.96$$"},{title:"Decide",body:"UCL_MR ≈ **1.96 ms**; LCL_MR = 0 (D₃ = 0 for n = 2)."}],final:"UCL_MR = 1.96 ms → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Used 2.66 (the I-chart factor) instead of D₄ = 3.267.","Divided the sum of ranges by 6 readings instead of by 5 moving ranges.","Used a factor of 3 instead of D₄ = 3.267.",""],bookRef:"Book p. 538"},{id:"sv-5-095",phase:5,topic:"5.2.2",qtype:"calculation",difficulty:3,stem:"An I-MR chart of **weekly churn (%)** has $\\bar{x}=2.40$ and $\\overline{MR}=0.30$. To apply the zone tests, where is the **upper boundary of Zone C** (one sigma above the centre line)?",options:["2.67","2.70","2.74","3.20"],answer:0,hint:"$$\\hat{\\sigma}=\\dfrac{\\overline{MR}}{d_2}=\\dfrac{\\overline{MR}}{1.128}$$ — Zone C ends at CL + 1σ̂.",steps:[{title:"Write what is given",body:"CL = 2.40, MR̄ = 0.30, d₂ = 1.128 for moving ranges of 2."},{title:"Estimate sigma",body:"$$\\hat{\\sigma}=\\dfrac{0.30}{1.128}=0.266$$"},{title:"Calculate the boundary",body:"$$CL+1\\hat{\\sigma}=2.40+0.266=2.67$$"},{title:"Decide",body:"Zone C runs from 2.13 to 2.67; Zone B from 2.67 to 2.93; Zone A from 2.93 to the UCL of 3.20."}],final:"Zone C upper boundary = 2.67% → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Used MR̄ itself as σ (forgot to divide by d₂).","Multiplied MR̄ by d₂ instead of dividing.","This is the UCL (CL + 3σ̂ = 2.40 + 2.66 × 0.30), the edge of Zone A, not Zone C."],bookRef:"Book pp. 534–538"},{id:"sv-5-096",phase:5,topic:"5.2.2",qtype:"output",difficulty:3,stem:`\`\`\`
I-MR Chart of Daily Port-Out Requests

Test Results for I Chart
TEST 1. One point more than 3.00 standard deviations from center line.
Test Failed at points:  14

Test Results for MR Chart
TEST 1. One point more than 3.00 standard deviations from center line.
Test Failed at points:  14, 15
\`\`\`

Records show that only **day 14** was unusual (a bulk port-out by one business customer). Why does the MR chart flag **both** 14 and 15?`,options:["The MR chart is more sensitive than the I chart, so it always flags one extra point","Day 15 must also contain a special cause that should be investigated separately","Both moving ranges MR₁₄ = |x₁₄ − x₁₃| and MR₁₅ = |x₁₅ − x₁₄| contain the unusual value, so one outlier inflates two ranges","Minitab applies Test 2 to the MR chart, which needs two points"],answer:2,hint:"Each moving range uses two consecutive values: $MR_i=|x_i-x_{i-1}|$.",steps:[{title:"Locate",body:"I chart: Test 1 at 14 only. MR chart: Test 1 at 14 and 15."},{title:"Apply",body:"Day 14 is far from day 13 (large MR₁₄) and day 15 returns to normal, far from day 14 (large MR₁₅). One unusual individual value therefore creates **two** large moving ranges."},{title:"Decide",body:"There is one special cause (day 14). Investigate it; day 15 does not need its own investigation."}],final:"One outlier inflates two consecutive moving ranges → answer **C**",whyWrong:["The MR chart does not “always flag an extra point”; the second flag is a consequence of how MRs are formed.","The second flag is caused by the same day-14 value; day 15 itself is normal.","","The output shows Test 1 on the MR chart, not Test 2."],bookRef:"Book pp. 518, 538"},{id:"sv-5-097",phase:5,topic:"5.2.2",qtype:"output",difficulty:2,stem:`\`\`\`
I-MR Chart of First-Contact Resolution (%)

Test Results for I Chart
TEST 2. 9 points in a row on same side of center line.
Test Failed at points:  20, 21, 22, 23

Test Results for MR Chart
No test failures.
\`\`\`

Which statement BEST describes the process?`,options:["Only point 20 is a special cause; points 21–23 are normal","Process variation has increased sharply from point 20","The MR chart is in control, so no action is needed","The process level shifted to one side of the centre line from about point 12, while point-to-point variation stayed stable"],answer:3,hint:"Test 2 flags the 9th point of a run; the run therefore started 8 points earlier.",steps:[{title:"Locate",body:"Test 2 first fails at point 20, the **ninth** point of the run, so the run started at point 20 − 8 = **12**. It continues through 23."},{title:"Read the MR chart",body:"No MR failures → the short-term spread is unchanged."},{title:"Decide",body:"A sustained shift in level began around point 12. Look for what changed at that time; if it is a desired improvement, confirm and standardise it."}],final:"Sustained shift from about point 12; spread stable → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Test 2 flags the 9th and later points of one continuous run — they share one cause that started earlier.","Test 2 detects a shift in level, and the MR chart shows no change in variation.","An in-control MR chart says nothing about a shift in level; the I chart shows one.",""],bookRef:"Book pp. 534–537"},{id:"sv-5-098",phase:5,topic:"5.2.2",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:2,stem:"Individual **fault-repair times** are strongly right-skewed. On the I chart, several points fall above the UCL every month and none ever come near the LCL, but investigations find no special causes. What should the team do FIRST?",options:["Respond to every point above the UCL as a special cause","Check the distribution; if it is non-normal, transform the data or chart subgroup averages before trusting the I-chart limits","Remove the high points from the data and recalculate the limits","Replace the I chart with a c chart"],answer:1,hint:"I-chart limits assume roughly normal data; skewness produces false alarms on the long tail.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book cautions that ±3σ limits contain 99.73% only for normal data (p. 525). With a long right tail, normal common-cause values exceed the UCL more often than expected."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"Repeated “signals” with no cause found, all on one side, point to skewness, not special causes. Test normality; then transform or use subgroup averages (which are closer to normal)."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Chasing every point is tampering; deleting valid data hides reality; a c chart is for counts of defects."}],final:"Check normality; transform or subgroup → answer **B**",whyWrong:["These are likely false alarms from skewed data; reacting to each is tampering.","","Deleting valid common-cause data makes the limits misleading.","Repair time is continuous; a c chart is for counts of defects."],bookRef:"Book pp. 525, 530–531"},{id:"sv-5-099",phase:5,topic:"5.2.3",qtype:"calculation",difficulty:1,stem:"A billing team times the processing of **3 consecutive invoices** every hour. After 25 subgroups, $\\bar{\\bar{x}}=82.0$ s and $\\bar{R}=6.0$ s. Constants: n = 3: A₂ = 1.023, D₄ = 2.574, d₂ = 1.693; n = 5: A₂ = 0.577. What is the **LCL of the Xbar chart**?",options:["78.54 s","75.86 s","71.37 s","64.00 s"],answer:1,hint:"$$LCL_{\\bar{x}}=\\bar{\\bar{x}}-A_2\\bar{R}$$ with A₂ for the subgroup size n.",steps:[{title:"Write what is given",body:"n = 3 (invoices per subgroup), $\\bar{\\bar{x}}$ = 82.0, R̄ = 6.0, A₂ = 1.023."},{title:"Pick the formula",body:"$$LCL_{\\bar{x}}=\\bar{\\bar{x}}-A_2\\bar{R}$$"},{title:"Substitute and calculate",body:"$$LCL_{\\bar{x}}=82.0-1.023\\times 6.0=82.0-6.138=75.86$$"},{title:"Decide",body:"LCL ≈ **75.86 s** (UCL ≈ 88.14 s)."}],final:"LCL = 75.86 s → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Used A₂ for n = 5 (0.577) instead of n = 3.","","Used ±3σ̂ for individual invoices (82 − 3 × 6 ÷ 1.693) — subgroup means need σ̂ ÷ √n, which A₂ already includes.","Used 3 × R̄, treating the range as if it were σ."],bookRef:"Book p. 538"},{id:"sv-5-100",phase:5,topic:"5.2.3",qtype:"calculation",difficulty:2,stem:`Five practice subgroups of **n = 4** cable-test times (min) are shown (a real Phase I study would use 20–25). Constants: n = 4: A₂ = 0.729, D₄ = 2.282; n = 5: D₄ = 2.114.

| Subgroup | 1 | 2 | 3 | 4 |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| A | 12 | 15 | 11 | 14 |
| B | 13 | 13 | 16 | 12 |
| C | 10 | 14 | 15 | 12 |
| D | 14 | 11 | 13 | 16 |
| E | 12 | 13 | 12 | 15 |

What is the **UCL of the R chart**?`,options:["12.60","9.58","8.88","3.06"],answer:1,hint:"$$\\bar{R}=\\dfrac{\\sum R_j}{k}\\qquad UCL_R=D_4\\bar{R}$$",steps:[{title:"Ranges",body:"R = max − min: A 15 − 11 = 4; B 16 − 12 = 4; C 15 − 10 = 5; D 16 − 11 = 5; E 15 − 12 = 3."},{title:"Average range",body:"$$\\bar{R}=\\dfrac{4+4+5+5+3}{5}=\\dfrac{21}{5}=4.2$$"},{title:"Calculate",body:"$$UCL_R=D_4\\bar{R}=2.282\\times 4.2=9.58$$"},{title:"Decide",body:"UCL_R ≈ **9.58 min**, LCL_R = 0 (D₃ = 0 for n = 4). All five ranges are inside."}],final:"UCL_R = 9.58 → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Used 3 × R̄ instead of D₄ × R̄.","","Used D₄ for n = 5 — the table has 5 subgroups, but the subgroup size is 4.","Used A₂ (the Xbar-chart constant) instead of D₄."],trap:"n is the number of values in a subgroup (4), not the number of subgroups (5).",bookRef:"Book p. 538"},{id:"sv-5-101",phase:5,topic:"5.2.3",qtype:"calculation",difficulty:3,stem:"An Xbar-R chart of **router reboot time** (s) uses subgroups of **n = 4** and shows **UCL = 105.2**, **CL = 100.0**, **LCL = 94.8** on the Xbar chart. Constants: n = 4: A₂ = 0.729; n = 5: A₂ = 0.577. What is $\\bar{R}$?",options:["14.27 s","9.01 s","7.13 s","3.79 s"],answer:2,hint:"$$UCL_{\\bar{x}}=\\bar{\\bar{x}}+A_2\\bar{R}\\ \\Rightarrow\\ \\bar{R}=\\dfrac{UCL_{\\bar{x}}-\\bar{\\bar{x}}}{A_2}$$",steps:[{title:"Write what is given",body:"UCL − CL = 105.2 − 100.0 = 5.2; n = 4 so A₂ = 0.729."},{title:"Rearrange",body:"$$\\bar{R}=\\dfrac{UCL_{\\bar{x}}-\\bar{\\bar{x}}}{A_2}$$"},{title:"Calculate",body:"$$\\bar{R}=\\dfrac{5.2}{0.729}=7.13$$"},{title:"Decide",body:"R̄ ≈ **7.13 s**; the R-chart UCL would be 2.282 × 7.13 ≈ 16.3 s."}],final:"R̄ = 7.13 s → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Divided the full width (UCL − LCL = 10.4) by A₂ instead of the half-width.","Used A₂ for n = 5 instead of n = 4.","","Multiplied the half-width by A₂ instead of dividing."],bookRef:"Book p. 538"},{id:"sv-5-102",phase:5,topic:"5.2.3",qtype:"calculation",difficulty:3,stem:"An Xbar-R chart of **daily average install time** (h) uses subgroups of **n = 5** and is stable with $\\bar{R}=4.652$ h. Using d₂ = 2.326, what is the estimated **standard deviation of the plotted subgroup averages**?",options:["0.40 h","0.89 h","2.00 h","2.68 h"],answer:1,hint:"$$\\hat{\\sigma}=\\dfrac{\\bar{R}}{d_2}\\qquad \\sigma_{\\bar{x}}=\\dfrac{\\hat{\\sigma}}{\\sqrt{n}}$$",steps:[{title:"Write what is given",body:"n = 5, R̄ = 4.652, d₂ = 2.326."},{title:"Sigma of individuals",body:"$$\\hat{\\sigma}=\\dfrac{4.652}{2.326}=2.00$$"},{title:"Sigma of subgroup means",body:"$$\\sigma_{\\bar{x}}=\\dfrac{2.00}{\\sqrt{5}}=\\dfrac{2.00}{2.236}=0.89$$"},{title:"Check",body:"$$3\\sigma_{\\bar{x}}=2.68=A_2\\bar{R}=0.577\\times 4.652$$ — the Xbar limits sit at ±2.68 h, consistent."}],final:"σ of subgroup means = 0.89 h → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Divided σ̂ by n instead of by √n.","","This is σ̂ of individual install times, not of the subgroup averages.","This is A₂R̄ = 3 standard errors (the half-width of the Xbar limits), not one."],bookRef:"Book pp. 519, 538"},{id:"sv-5-103",phase:5,topic:"5.2.3",qtype:"output",difficulty:3,stem:`A colleague sends this extract but forgot to note the subgroup size:

\`\`\`
Xbar-R Chart of Fibre Splice Time (min)

Xbar Chart:  UCL = 50.94   CL = 48.20   LCL = 45.46
R Chart:     UCL = 8.58    CL = 3.76    LCL = 0
\`\`\`

Constants: n = 3: A₂ 1.023, D₄ 2.574 · n = 4: A₂ 0.729, D₄ 2.282 · n = 5: A₂ 0.577, D₄ 2.114 · n = 6: A₂ 0.483, D₄ 2.004. What subgroup size was used?`,options:["n = 3","n = 4","n = 5","n = 6"],answer:1,hint:"$$A_2=\\dfrac{UCL_{\\bar{x}}-\\bar{\\bar{x}}}{\\bar{R}}\\qquad D_4=\\dfrac{UCL_R}{\\bar{R}}$$ — match to the table.",steps:[{title:"Locate",body:"Xbar half-width = 50.94 − 48.20 = 2.74; R̄ = 3.76; UCL_R = 8.58."},{title:"Back out A₂",body:"$$A_2=\\dfrac{2.74}{3.76}=0.729$$"},{title:"Back out D₄",body:"$$D_4=\\dfrac{8.58}{3.76}=2.282$$"},{title:"Decide",body:"A₂ = 0.729 and D₄ = 2.282 both match **n = 4**."}],final:"A₂ = 0.729, D₄ = 2.282 → n = 4 → answer **B**",whyWrong:["n = 3 would give A₂R̄ = 1.023 × 3.76 = 3.85, an Xbar UCL of 52.05.","","n = 5 would give A₂R̄ = 0.577 × 3.76 = 2.17, an Xbar UCL of 50.37.","n = 6 would give A₂R̄ = 0.483 × 3.76 = 1.82, an Xbar UCL of 50.02."],bookRef:"Book p. 538"},{id:"sv-5-104",phase:5,topic:"5.2.3",qtype:"output",difficulty:3,stem:`\`\`\`
Xbar-R Chart of Handover Time (min)        n = 4, 25 subgroups
Data recorded to the nearest whole minute

R Chart:     UCL = 0.82    CL (R-bar) = 0.36    LCL = 0
             Subgroups with R = 0: 14 of 25
Xbar Chart:  TEST 1 failed at points 2, 5, 6, 9, 11, 14, 17, 18, 21, 24
\`\`\`

What is the MOST likely explanation?`,options:["The process is badly out of control and should be stopped until all ten causes are found","The subgroup size of 4 is too large","The R chart is in control, so all ten Xbar signals are genuine special causes","Measurement resolution is too coarse: many zero ranges make R̄ too small, so the Xbar limits are too narrow and produce false signals"],answer:3,hint:"Most subgroups with R = 0 is a sign of inadequate measurement discrimination; R̄ then underestimates σ.",steps:[{title:"Locate",body:"14 of 25 subgroups have R = 0, and the data are rounded to whole minutes. R̄ = 0.36 min is tiny."},{title:"Apply",body:"Rounding hides real within-subgroup variation, so R̄ underestimates σ. The Xbar limits $\\bar{\\bar{x}}\\pm A_2\\bar{R}$ become far too narrow, and normal subgroup-to-subgroup variation looks like special causes."},{title:"Decide",body:"Fix the measurement first — record to a finer resolution (seconds) — and rebuild the chart. This is an MSA (discrimination) problem, not ten special causes."}],final:"Coarse resolution → R̄ too small → false Xbar signals → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Ten “signals” with this many zero ranges are a measurement artefact, not ten separate special causes.","n = 4 is a normal Xbar-R subgroup size; rounding is the problem.","An R chart “in control” on rounded data is meaningless; its R̄ is understated.",""],bookRef:"Book pp. 519, 538"},{id:"sv-5-105",phase:5,topic:"5.2.3",qtype:"concept",difficulty:2,stem:"On an Xbar-R chart, what does the quantity $A_2\\bar{R}$ represent?",options:["One standard deviation of individual values","Three standard errors of the subgroup mean, i.e. the distance from the centre line to each Xbar limit","The upper control limit of the R chart","The specification tolerance divided by six"],answer:1,hint:"$$A_2=\\dfrac{3}{d_2\\sqrt{n}}\\ \\Rightarrow\\ A_2\\bar{R}=3\\times\\dfrac{\\bar{R}}{d_2\\sqrt{n}}$$",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"$$A_2\\bar{R}=3\\times\\dfrac{\\hat{\\sigma}}{\\sqrt{n}}=3\\sigma_{\\bar{x}}$$ — A₂ combines the conversion from R̄ to σ̂ (÷d₂), the ÷√n for averages, and the factor 3."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"σ̂ of individuals is R̄ ÷ d₂; the R-chart UCL is D₄R̄; control limits are never built from the specification."}],final:"A₂R̄ = 3σ of the subgroup mean → answer **B**",whyWrong:["σ̂ of individuals is R̄ ÷ d₂, not A₂R̄.","","The R-chart UCL uses D₄, not A₂.","Control limits come from process data, not from the specification."],bookRef:"Book p. 538"},{id:"sv-5-106",phase:5,topic:"5.2.4",qtype:"calculation",difficulty:1,stem:"A weekly security scan of the **same 60-server estate** finds a total of **216** vulnerabilities over **24 weeks**. Using a **c chart**, what are the control limits?",options:["UCL = 12, LCL = 6","UCL = 15, LCL = 3","UCL = 18, LCL = 0","UCL = 36, LCL = 0"],answer:2,hint:"$$\\bar{c}=\\dfrac{\\text{total defects}}{\\text{number of subgroups}}\\qquad \\bar{c}\\pm 3\\sqrt{\\bar{c}}$$",steps:[{title:"Write what is given",body:"Constant area of opportunity (same 60 servers) → c chart. 24 weeks, 216 defects."},{title:"Centre line",body:"$$\\bar{c}=\\dfrac{216}{24}=9$$"},{title:"Limits",body:`$$UCL=\\bar{c}+3\\sqrt{\\bar{c}}=9+3\\times 3=18$$

$$LCL=9-3\\times 3=0$$`},{title:"Decide",body:"UCL = **18**, LCL = **0** vulnerabilities per weekly scan."}],final:"UCL = 18, LCL = 0 → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Used ±1√c̄ (forgot the factor 3): 9 ± 3.","Used ±2√c̄ (2-sigma limits): 9 ± 6.","","Forgot the square root: 9 + 3 × 9 = 36."],bookRef:"Book p. 541"},{id:"sv-5-107",phase:5,topic:"5.2.4",qtype:"calculation",difficulty:2,stem:"A u chart tracks **configuration defects per change request** with $\\bar{u}=1.2$. Today **30** change requests were reviewed. What is today’s **LCL**?",options:["0","0.60","0.80","1.08"],answer:1,hint:"$$LCL_i=\\bar{u}-3\\sqrt{\\dfrac{\\bar{u}}{n_i}}$$ (set to 0 if negative).",steps:[{title:"Write what is given",body:"ū = 1.2 defects per request, nᵢ = 30."},{title:"Pick the formula",body:"$$LCL_i=\\bar{u}-3\\sqrt{\\dfrac{\\bar{u}}{n_i}}$$"},{title:"Substitute and calculate",body:"$$LCL=1.2-3\\sqrt{\\dfrac{1.2}{30}}=1.2-3\\times 0.2=0.60$$"},{title:"Decide",body:"LCL = **0.60** defects per request; a day below this would signal an unusually good day worth investigating."}],final:"LCL = 0.60 → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Used the c-chart formula ū − 3√ū (negative, so set to 0) and ignored the subgroup size.","","Used 2-sigma instead of 3-sigma limits: 1.2 − 2 × 0.2.","Forgot the square root: 1.2 − 3 × (1.2 ÷ 30) = 1.08."],bookRef:"Book p. 540"},{id:"sv-5-108",phase:5,topic:"5.2.4",qtype:"calculation",difficulty:2,stem:`A fibre-installation audit counts **defects** (an installation can have several).

| Day | Mon | Tue | Wed | Thu | Fri |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| Installations audited | 20 | 35 | 25 | 40 | 30 |
| Defects found | 14 | 18 | 16 | 20 | 22 |

Using a u chart, what is **Monday’s UCL**?`,options:["0.69","1.02","1.12","2.92"],answer:2,hint:"$$\\bar{u}=\\dfrac{\\sum c_i}{\\sum n_i}\\qquad UCL_i=\\bar{u}+3\\sqrt{\\dfrac{\\bar{u}}{n_i}}$$",steps:[{title:"Centre line",body:"$$\\bar{u}=\\dfrac{14+18+16+20+22}{20+35+25+40+30}=\\dfrac{90}{150}=0.60$$"},{title:"Monday’s limit",body:"$$UCL_{Mon}=0.60+3\\sqrt{\\dfrac{0.60}{20}}=0.60+3\\times 0.173=1.12$$"},{title:"Decide",body:"Monday’s UCL = **1.12** defects per installation. Monday’s u = 14 ÷ 20 = 0.70 is inside."}],final:"UCL_Mon = 1.12 → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Forgot the square root: 0.60 + 3 × (0.60 ÷ 20) = 0.69.","Used the average subgroup size (30) instead of Monday’s 20 installations.","","Left out nᵢ (c-chart style): 0.60 + 3√0.60 = 2.92."],trap:"On a u chart each day has its own limits: use that day’s nᵢ.",bookRef:"Book p. 540"},{id:"sv-5-109",phase:5,topic:"5.2.4",qtype:"calculation",difficulty:3,stem:`A c chart counts **dropped-call alarms per day** on one fixed cluster of cell sites, with $\\bar{c}=2.25$. The last six days were:

**3, 6, 7, 2, 0, 8**

How many of these days signal under Test 1?`,options:["4","3","2","0"],answer:2,hint:"$$UCL=\\bar{c}+3\\sqrt{\\bar{c}}\\qquad LCL=\\bar{c}-3\\sqrt{\\bar{c}}\\ (\\text{0 if negative})$$",steps:[{title:"Limits",body:`$$UCL=2.25+3\\sqrt{2.25}=2.25+3\\times 1.5=6.75$$

$$LCL=2.25-4.5<0\\ \\Rightarrow\\ 0$$`},{title:"Compare each day",body:"3, 6, 2 and 0 are inside; **7** and **8** are above 6.75. A count of 0 cannot be below an LCL of 0."},{title:"Decide",body:"**2 days** signal (the days with 7 and 8 alarms)."}],final:"2 days signal → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Also flagged the 0 as below the LCL — the LCL is set to 0 and a point at 0 is not beyond it.","Used 2-sigma limits (UCL = 5.25), which also flags the 6.","","Forgot the square root: UCL = 2.25 + 3 × 2.25 = 9, so nothing appears to signal."],bookRef:"Book p. 541"},{id:"sv-5-110",phase:5,topic:"5.2.4",qtype:"output",difficulty:2,stem:`\`\`\`
U Chart of Defects per Installation      U-bar = 1.100
Tests performed with unequal sample sizes

Point   Installations   Defects     U       UCL
  8          32            38     1.19     1.66
  9           6            19     3.17     2.38
 10          28            34     1.21     1.69

TEST 1. One point more than 3.00 standard deviations from center line.
Test Failed at points:  9
\`\`\`

Which statement is correct?`,options:["Point 9 is above its own UCL, which is wider because the subgroup is small; it is a genuine signal to investigate","Point 9 should be compared with the UCL of point 8 (1.66), so it signals even more strongly","Point 9 can be ignored because it is based on only 6 installations","All points should share one UCL, so the chart is set up incorrectly"],answer:0,hint:"$$UCL_i=\\bar{u}+3\\sqrt{\\dfrac{\\bar{u}}{n_i}}$$ — small nᵢ gives a wide limit, already allowing for the small sample.",steps:[{title:"Locate",body:"Point 9: u = 19 ÷ 6 = 3.17, its UCL = 2.38."},{title:"Check the limit",body:"$$UCL_9=1.1+3\\sqrt{\\dfrac{1.1}{6}}=1.1+3\\times 0.428=2.38$$ — wider than for points 8 and 10 because n is small."},{title:"Decide",body:"Even with its wide limit, point 9 is beyond it, so it is a real Test 1 signal. Follow the response plan for that day."}],final:"Point 9 exceeds its own (wider) UCL → investigate → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Each point must be judged against its own limit; point 8’s UCL is for 32 installations.","The small-sample effect is already built into the wider UCL; it still signals.","Stepped limits are correct for a u chart when the number of units varies (book p. 540)."],bookRef:"Book p. 540"},{id:"sv-5-111",phase:5,topic:"5.2.4",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:1,stem:"A team has been using a **c chart** of defects found per weekly audit of **50 street cabinets**. From next month the number of cabinets audited will vary between 30 and 80 each week. What should the team do?",options:["Keep the c chart; the limits will still be valid","Switch to a p chart of the proportion of defective cabinets","Switch to an I-MR chart of the weekly defect count","Switch to a u chart of defects per cabinet, with limits that vary with each week’s count"],answer:3,hint:"c chart needs a constant area of opportunity; when it varies, use u (defects per unit).",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book notes that a C chart needs a constant sample size, while a U chart handles equal or unequal subgroups (pp. 520, 531)."},{title:"Apply",body:"With 30–80 cabinets per week, raw counts are not comparable. Defects per cabinet on a u chart, with nᵢ-based limits, keeps the data as defects and adjusts for size."}],final:"Switch to a u chart → answer **D**",whyWrong:["c-chart limits assume the same area of opportunity each week; an 80-cabinet week would look worse simply because it is bigger.","A p chart counts defective units, not defects; it would lose the information on multiple defects per cabinet.","An I-MR chart of raw counts still ignores the changing number of cabinets.",""],bookRef:"Book pp. 520, 531"},{id:"sv-5-112",phase:5,topic:"5.2.5",qtype:"calculation",difficulty:1,stem:"A p chart tracks **failed eSIM downloads** in a constant daily sample of **500** downloads, with $\\bar{p}=0.06$. What is the **LCL**?",options:["0.0281","0.0494","0.0597","0.0919"],answer:0,hint:"$$LCL=\\bar{p}-3\\sqrt{\\dfrac{\\bar{p}(1-\\bar{p})}{n}}$$",steps:[{title:"Write what is given",body:"p̄ = 0.06, n = 500."},{title:"Standard error",body:"$$\\sqrt{\\dfrac{0.06\\times 0.94}{500}}=\\sqrt{0.0001128}=0.01062$$"},{title:"Calculate",body:"$$LCL=0.06-3\\times 0.01062=0.0281$$"},{title:"Decide",body:"LCL ≈ **0.0281** (2.81%); UCL ≈ 0.0919."}],final:"LCL = 0.0281 → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Used 1 standard error instead of 3.","Forgot the square root: 0.06 − 3 × 0.0001128.","This is the UCL, not the LCL."],bookRef:"Book p. 539"},{id:"sv-5-113",phase:5,topic:"5.2.5",qtype:"calculation",difficulty:2,stem:`A team audits activation orders for errors (each order pass/fail):

| Day | Orders audited | Defective orders |
|---|---|---|
| 1 | 200 | 10 |
| 2 | 50 | 6 |
| 3 | 400 | 16 |
| 4 | 150 | 9 |

What is the **centre line** ($\\bar{p}$) of the p chart?`,options:["10.25","0.2700","0.0675","0.0513"],answer:3,hint:"$$\\bar{p}=\\dfrac{\\sum d_i}{\\sum n_i}$$ — pool, do not average the daily proportions.",steps:[{title:"Write what is given",body:"Total defectives = 10 + 6 + 16 + 9 = 41; total audited = 200 + 50 + 400 + 150 = 800."},{title:"Pick the formula",body:"$$\\bar{p}=\\dfrac{\\sum d_i}{\\sum n_i}$$"},{title:"Calculate",body:"$$\\bar{p}=\\dfrac{41}{800}=0.0513$$"},{title:"Decide",body:"p̄ ≈ **0.0513**. Pooling weights each day by its size; averaging the daily rates would let the 50-order day count as much as the 400-order day."}],final:"p̄ = 0.0513 → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Averaged the defective counts per day (41 ÷ 4) — that is a count, not a proportion.","Added the four daily proportions without dividing by 4.","Averaged the daily proportions (0.05, 0.12, 0.04, 0.06), giving the small day too much weight.",""],bookRef:"Book p. 539"},{id:"sv-5-114",phase:5,topic:"5.2.5",qtype:"calculation",difficulty:3,stem:"A p chart of **failed fibre activations** has $\\bar{p}=0.05$. Today **240** activations were attempted and **22** failed. Which conclusion is correct?",options:["UCL = 0.0506; p = 0.0917 is above it, so today signals","UCL = 0.0781; p = 0.0917 is above it, so today signals","UCL = 0.0922; 22 failures is far above it, so today signals","UCL = 0.0922; p = 0.0917 is just inside it, so there is no Test 1 signal"],answer:3,hint:"$$UCL=\\bar{p}+3\\sqrt{\\dfrac{\\bar{p}(1-\\bar{p})}{n_i}}$$ and plot $p_i=\\dfrac{d_i}{n_i}$.",steps:[{title:"Plotted point",body:"$$p=\\dfrac{22}{240}=0.0917$$"},{title:"Today’s UCL",body:"$$UCL=0.05+3\\sqrt{\\dfrac{0.05\\times 0.95}{240}}=0.05+3\\times 0.01407=0.0922$$"},{title:"Decide",body:"0.0917 < 0.0922, so the point is just **inside** the limit — no Test 1 signal. (Check the run tests before concluding the day is unremarkable.)"}],final:"p = 0.0917 < UCL 0.0922 → no Test 1 signal → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Forgot the square root in the standard error.","Used 2-sigma limits instead of 3-sigma.","Compared the count of 22 with a limit expressed as a proportion.",""],trap:"Compare like with like: proportion with a proportion limit.",bookRef:"Book p. 539"},{id:"sv-5-115",phase:5,topic:"5.2.5",qtype:"calculation",difficulty:3,stem:"A p chart of **late repair visits** has $\\bar{p}=0.04$, and the daily sample size varies. On which of these days would an observed proportion of **0.070** fall **above** the UCL?",options:["A day with n = 200","A day with n = 300","A day with n = 350","A day with n = 400"],answer:3,hint:"$$UCL_i=\\bar{p}+3\\sqrt{\\dfrac{\\bar{p}(1-\\bar{p})}{n_i}}$$ — larger nᵢ → tighter limit.",steps:[{title:"Pick the formula",body:"$$UCL_i=0.04+3\\sqrt{\\dfrac{0.04\\times 0.96}{n_i}}$$"},{title:"Calculate each limit",body:`$$UCL_{200}=0.04+3\\sqrt{\\dfrac{0.0384}{200}}=0.0816$$

$$UCL_{300}=0.04+3\\sqrt{\\dfrac{0.0384}{300}}=0.0739$$

$$UCL_{350}=0.04+3\\sqrt{\\dfrac{0.0384}{350}}=0.0714$$

$$UCL_{400}=0.04+3\\sqrt{\\dfrac{0.0384}{400}}=0.0694$$`},{title:"Decide",body:"Only at n = 400 is 0.070 above the UCL (0.0694)."}],final:"Only n = 400 (UCL 0.0694) → answer **D**",whyWrong:["UCL = 0.0816; a small sample has a wide limit.","UCL = 0.0739; 0.070 is still inside.","UCL = 0.0714; 0.070 is just inside.",""],bookRef:"Book p. 539"},{id:"sv-5-116",phase:5,topic:"5.2.5",qtype:"output",difficulty:3,stem:`\`\`\`
P Chart of Failed Number Ports        P-bar = 0.0400
Tests performed with unequal sample sizes

Point     n     Failed      P        UCL
   5     150      12     0.0800    0.0880
  12     900      55     0.0611    0.0596

TEST 1. One point more than 3.00 standard deviations from center line.
Test Failed at points:  12
\`\`\`

A manager asks why point 12 signals when its proportion (6.1%) is **lower** than point 5 (8.0%), which did not signal. Which answer is correct?`,options:["It is a Minitab error; point 5 has the higher proportion and should be the one flagged","Point 12 is based on a much larger sample, so its limit is tighter; 0.0611 is above its own UCL of 0.0596, while 0.0800 is inside point 5’s wider UCL of 0.0880","Point 12 signals because 55 failures is more than 12 failures","Point 12 signals under Test 2, not Test 1"],answer:1,hint:"$$UCL_i=\\bar{p}+3\\sqrt{\\dfrac{\\bar{p}(1-\\bar{p})}{n_i}}$$ — the limit depends on each subgroup’s n.",steps:[{title:"Locate",body:"Point 5: n = 150, p = 0.0800, UCL = 0.0880. Point 12: n = 900, p = 0.0611, UCL = 0.0596."},{title:"Check the limits",body:`$$UCL_{5}=0.04+3\\sqrt{\\dfrac{0.0384}{150}}=0.0880$$

$$UCL_{12}=0.04+3\\sqrt{\\dfrac{0.0384}{900}}=0.0596$$`},{title:"Decide",body:"Each point is judged against its own limit. With 900 ports, 6.1% is very unlikely by chance; with 150, 8.0% is not."}],final:"Larger n → tighter UCL; point 12 exceeds its own limit → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Minitab is right: each point is compared with its own nᵢ-based limit.","","The chart plots proportions, not counts; the count comparison is irrelevant.","The output shows Test 1 (beyond 3σ), not Test 2."],bookRef:"Book pp. 521, 539"},{id:"sv-5-117",phase:5,topic:"5.2.5",qtype:"concept",difficulty:1,stem:"A **p chart** is the appropriate attribute chart when:",options:["each unit is classified as defective or not defective, and the subgroup size may vary","defects are counted on units of a constant size","each unit is measured on a continuous scale","the number of defects per unit is tracked with a varying number of units"],answer:0,hint:"Defectives (binomial) → p (any n) / np (constant n); defects (Poisson) → c / u.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book describes the P chart as tracking the proportion nonconforming for **equal or unequal** subgroups, for pass/fail (defective) data — the most common attribute chart (pp. 521, 531)."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Counting defects on a constant unit is the c chart; defects per unit with varying size is the u chart; continuous data use variables charts."}],final:"Defectives, any subgroup size → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","That describes a c chart (defects, constant area of opportunity).","Continuous measurements use I-MR, Xbar-R or Xbar-S charts.","That describes a u chart (defects per unit)."],bookRef:"Book pp. 521, 531"},{id:"sv-5-118",phase:5,topic:"5.2.6",qtype:"calculation",difficulty:1,stem:"An np chart counts **defective work orders** in a fixed daily sample of **120**. Over **30 days**, **216** defective orders were found. What is the **UCL**?",options:["9.80","15.00","15.25","27.50"],answer:1,hint:"$$n\\bar{p}=\\dfrac{\\text{total defectives}}{\\text{number of subgroups}}\\qquad UCL=n\\bar{p}+3\\sqrt{n\\bar{p}(1-\\bar{p})}$$",steps:[{title:"Centre line",body:"$$n\\bar{p}=\\dfrac{216}{30}=7.2\\qquad \\bar{p}=\\dfrac{7.2}{120}=0.06$$"},{title:"Standard deviation",body:"$$\\sqrt{n\\bar{p}(1-\\bar{p})}=\\sqrt{7.2\\times 0.94}=2.601$$"},{title:"Calculate",body:"$$UCL=7.2+3\\times 2.601=15.00$$"},{title:"Decide",body:"UCL ≈ **15.0** defective orders per day (LCL is negative → 0)."}],final:"UCL = 15.00 → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Used 1 standard deviation instead of 3.","","Forgot the (1 − p̄) term (Poisson instead of binomial): 7.2 + 3√7.2.","Forgot the square root: 7.2 + 3 × 6.768."],bookRef:"Book p. 540"},{id:"sv-5-119",phase:5,topic:"5.2.6",qtype:"calculation",difficulty:2,stem:"An np chart of **rejected port-in requests** uses a constant sample of **n = 400** a day with $\\bar{p}=0.10$. What is the **LCL**?",options:["0","21.0","22.0","34.0"],answer:2,hint:"$$LCL=n\\bar{p}-3\\sqrt{n\\bar{p}(1-\\bar{p})}$$ (0 only if negative).",steps:[{title:"Write what is given",body:"n = 400, p̄ = 0.10, so n p̄ = 40."},{title:"Standard deviation",body:"$$\\sqrt{n\\bar{p}(1-\\bar{p})}=\\sqrt{40\\times 0.90}=\\sqrt{36}=6$$"},{title:"Calculate",body:"$$LCL=40-3\\times 6=22$$"},{title:"Decide",body:"LCL = **22** rejected requests (UCL = 58). A day with fewer than 22 rejects is a signal worth investigating."}],final:"LCL = 22 → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Assumed the LCL is always 0 for attribute charts; here n p̄ is large enough for a positive LCL.","Forgot the (1 − p̄) term: 40 − 3√40 = 21.0.","","Used 1 standard deviation instead of 3: 40 − 6."],bookRef:"Book p. 540"},{id:"sv-5-120",phase:5,topic:"5.2.6",qtype:"calculation",difficulty:3,stem:`An np chart monitors **failed site-acceptance tests** in a fixed sample of **n = 250** per week, with $\\bar{p}=0.08$.

| Week | 1 | 2 | 3 | 4 | 5 | 6 |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| Failed | 6 | 18 | 33 | 25 | 8 | 31 |

How many weeks fall **outside** the control limits?`,options:["0","1","2","4"],answer:2,hint:"$$n\\bar{p}\\pm 3\\sqrt{n\\bar{p}(1-\\bar{p})}$$",steps:[{title:"Centre line",body:"$$n\\bar{p}=250\\times 0.08=20$$"},{title:"Limits",body:"$$\\sqrt{20\\times 0.92}=4.29\\qquad UCL=20+3\\times 4.29=32.87\\qquad LCL=20-3\\times 4.29=7.13$$"},{title:"Compare",body:"Week 1 (6) is **below** the LCL; week 3 (33) is **above** the UCL. Weeks 2, 4, 5 (8 > 7.13) and 6 (31) are inside."},{title:"Decide",body:"**2 weeks** are outside the limits. Week 1 is a favourable signal — find out what went right."}],final:"2 weeks (1 and 3) → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Forgot the square root: 20 ± 3 × 18.4 = 20 ± 55.2, so nothing appears to be outside.","Checked only the UCL and ignored the positive LCL, missing week 1.","","Used 2-sigma limits (11.42 to 28.58), which also flags weeks 5 and 6."],bookRef:"Book p. 540"},{id:"sv-5-121",phase:5,topic:"5.2.6",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:1,stem:"A quality lead audits **exactly 50 installation photo sets** per day and marks each set pass or fail. Supervisors want a chart that shows the **number** of failed sets, because technicians find counts easier to understand than percentages. Which chart fits BEST?",options:["np chart","c chart","u chart","Xbar-R chart"],answer:0,hint:"Count of defective units with a constant sample size → np chart.",steps:[{title:"Classify the data",body:"Each photo set is pass/fail → defectives; the sample is a constant 50 per day."},{title:"Pick the chart",body:"The book’s selection table: NP tracks the **number** of defective units with a constant sample size (p. 531). A p chart would also be valid but plots proportions."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"c and u charts are for defects (flaws); Xbar-R is for continuous data."}],final:"np chart → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","A c chart counts defects in a constant area, not defective units.","A u chart is for defects per unit with varying size.","Pass/fail data are attribute data, not continuous measurements."],bookRef:"Book pp. 530–531"},{id:"sv-5-122",phase:5,topic:"5.2.6",qtype:"table",difficulty:2,stem:`A team has run an np chart (limits set for **n = 200**) on defective change records. The sampling plan has since changed:

| Week | Records checked | Defective |
|---|---|---|
| 1 | 200 | 9 |
| 2 | 200 | 12 |
| 3 | 300 | 14 |
| 4 | 300 | 18 |

What should the team do?`,options:["Keep the np chart and plot weeks 3 and 4 against the n = 200 limits","Keep the np chart, using the average sample size of 250 for all weeks","Switch to a c chart of defects per week","Switch to a p chart (or rebuild the np limits for n = 300), because np limits are only valid for a constant n"],answer:3,hint:"np limits $n\\bar{p}\\pm 3\\sqrt{n\\bar{p}(1-\\bar{p})}$ depend on n; when n changes, use p or recompute.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"An np chart plots counts, so its centre line n p̄ and its limits depend directly on n. A count of 18 from 300 records is not comparable with a count from 200."},{title:"Apply",body:"With n = 300, the old centre line (n p̄ for 200) understates the expected count, so weeks 3–4 would look worse simply because more records were checked."},{title:"Decide",body:"Plot proportions on a p chart (handles any n), or rebuild the np chart for the new constant n = 300."}],final:"Switch to p (or rebuild the np limits for n = 300) → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Larger samples produce larger counts; judging them against n = 200 limits creates false signals.","An average n is wrong for every week — the limits must match each actual n.","Defective records are defectives (pass/fail), not defects; a c chart also needs a constant area.",""],bookRef:"Book pp. 521, 540"},{id:"sv-5-123",phase:5,topic:"5.2.7",qtype:"calculation",difficulty:1,stem:"An Xbar-S chart of **page-load time** (ms) uses subgroups of **n = 9** with $\\bar{\\bar{x}}=250$ ms and $\\bar{s}=6$ ms. Constants: n = 9: A₂ = 0.337, A₃ = 1.032, B₄ = 1.761; n = 5: A₃ = 1.427. What is the **UCL of the Xbar chart**?",options:["252.02 ms","256.19 ms","258.56 ms","268.00 ms"],answer:1,hint:"$$UCL_{\\bar{x}}=\\bar{\\bar{x}}+A_3\\bar{s}$$",steps:[{title:"Write what is given",body:"n = 9, $\\bar{\\bar{x}}$ = 250, s̄ = 6, A₃ = 1.032."},{title:"Pick the formula",body:"$$UCL_{\\bar{x}}=\\bar{\\bar{x}}+A_3\\bar{s}$$"},{title:"Substitute and calculate",body:"$$UCL=250+1.032\\times 6=250+6.19=256.19$$"},{title:"Decide",body:"UCL ≈ **256.19 ms**; LCL ≈ 243.81 ms."}],final:"UCL = 256.19 ms → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Used A₂ (the Xbar-R constant for R̄) with s̄.","","Used A₃ for n = 5 instead of n = 9.","Used 3 × s̄, ignoring that subgroup means vary less than individuals."],bookRef:"Book p. 539"},{id:"sv-5-124",phase:5,topic:"5.2.7",qtype:"calculation",difficulty:2,stem:"An Xbar-S chart of **ticket-triage time** (min) uses subgroups of **n = 7** and is stable with $\\bar{s}=3.358$ min. Constants for n = 7: c₄ = 0.9594, d₂ = 2.704. What is the estimated process standard deviation $\\hat{\\sigma}$?",options:["1.24 min","3.22 min","3.36 min","3.50 min"],answer:3,hint:"$$\\hat{\\sigma}=\\dfrac{\\bar{s}}{c_4}$$",steps:[{title:"Write what is given",body:"s̄ = 3.358, c₄ = 0.9594 for n = 7."},{title:"Pick the formula",body:"The average subgroup s slightly underestimates σ, so divide by c₄: $$\\hat{\\sigma}=\\dfrac{\\bar{s}}{c_4}$$"},{title:"Calculate",body:"$$\\hat{\\sigma}=\\dfrac{3.358}{0.9594}=3.50$$"},{title:"Decide",body:"σ̂ ≈ **3.50 min** — the within-subgroup σ used for Cp and Cpk."}],final:"σ̂ = 3.50 min → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Divided s̄ by d₂, the constant for ranges, not standard deviations.","Multiplied by c₄ instead of dividing.","Used s̄ uncorrected; s̄ is biased low and must be divided by c₄.",""],bookRef:"Book p. 539"},{id:"sv-5-125",phase:5,topic:"5.2.7",qtype:"calculation",difficulty:3,stem:"An S chart of **API response time** uses subgroups of **n = 10** with $\\bar{s}=2.0$ ms. Three new subgroups have standard deviations of **0.45, 1.90 and 3.50** ms. Constants: n = 10: B₃ = 0.284, B₄ = 1.716, D₃ = 0.223, D₄ = 1.777; n = 5: B₃ = 0, B₄ = 2.089. Which values signal?",options:["None of them","Only 0.45","Only 3.50","0.45 and 3.50"],answer:3,hint:"$$LCL_s=B_3\\bar{s}\\qquad UCL_s=B_4\\bar{s}$$ with constants for the subgroup size.",steps:[{title:"Limits",body:"$$LCL_s=0.284\\times 2.0=0.568\\qquad UCL_s=1.716\\times 2.0=3.432$$"},{title:"Compare",body:"0.45 < 0.568 → below the LCL; 1.90 inside; 3.50 > 3.432 → above the UCL."},{title:"Decide",body:"**0.45 and 3.50** both signal. The low one shows unusually small variation — investigate it too (a stuck sensor, or a better method worth keeping)."}],final:"0.45 and 3.50 signal → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Used D₃ and D₄ (range constants), giving 0.446 to 3.554, so nothing appears to signal.","Used B₄ for n = 5 (2.089), giving a UCL of 4.18, so 3.50 looks inside.","Assumed the S-chart LCL is 0 (true only for n ≤ 5), missing the low point.",""],bookRef:"Book p. 539"},{id:"sv-5-126",phase:5,topic:"5.2.7",qtype:"calculation",difficulty:2,stem:"An Xbar-S chart of **call-setup time** (ms) uses subgroups of **n = 6**. The Xbar chart shows **UCL = 53.861** and **LCL = 46.139**. Constants for n = 6: A₂ = 0.483, A₃ = 1.287. What is $\\bar{s}$?",options:["1.29 ms","3.00 ms","6.00 ms","7.99 ms"],answer:1,hint:"$$UCL-LCL=2A_3\\bar{s}\\ \\Rightarrow\\ \\bar{s}=\\dfrac{UCL-LCL}{2A_3}$$",steps:[{title:"Write what is given",body:"Width = 53.861 − 46.139 = 7.722; A₃ = 1.287."},{title:"Rearrange",body:"$$\\bar{s}=\\dfrac{UCL-LCL}{2A_3}$$"},{title:"Calculate",body:"$$\\bar{s}=\\dfrac{7.722}{2\\times 1.287}=\\dfrac{7.722}{2.574}=3.00$$"},{title:"Decide",body:"s̄ = **3.00 ms**."}],final:"s̄ = 3.00 ms → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Divided the width by 6, treating it as ±3σ of individuals.","","Divided the full width by A₃ without the factor 2 (used the width as if it were the half-width).","Used A₂ (the Xbar-R constant) instead of A₃."],bookRef:"Book p. 539"},{id:"sv-5-127",phase:5,topic:"5.2.7",qtype:"output",difficulty:2,stem:`\`\`\`
Xbar-S Chart of API Response Time (ms)      n = 10

S Chart:   UCL = 5.15   CL (S-bar) = 3.00   LCL = 0.85
TEST 1. One point more than 3.00 standard deviations from center line.
Test Failed at points:  31     (S = 0.62)

Xbar Chart: no test failures
\`\`\`

What is the BEST interpretation?`,options:["Ignore it: a point below the LCL on an S chart is good news and needs no action","Variation within subgroup 31 was unusually low — a special cause; investigate (for example a stuck monitoring probe or a new method) and keep it if it is a real improvement","The S-chart LCL should be 0, so the point is a false alarm","The Xbar chart has no failures, so the S-chart signal does not matter"],answer:1,hint:"A point below the LCL of a spread chart = unusually small variation; it is still a special cause.",steps:[{title:"Locate",body:"S chart, Test 1 at subgroup 31: s = 0.62 < LCL = 0.85 (= B₃s̄ = 0.284 × 3.00)."},{title:"Apply",body:"With n = 10, B₃ > 0, so the S chart has a real lower limit. A value below it means much less variation than common cause allows."},{title:"Decide",body:"Investigate: either a measurement problem (a probe returning near-constant values) or a genuine improvement to standardise."}],final:"Unusually low variation — investigate and keep if real → answer **B**",whyWrong:["A favourable signal is still a special cause: find out why, so it can be kept — or so a measurement fault is caught.","","For n = 10, B₃ = 0.284, so the S-chart LCL is genuinely above 0.","The S chart is read first; a spread signal matters regardless of the Xbar chart."],bookRef:"Book pp. 532, 539"},{id:"sv-5-128",phase:5,topic:"5.2.8",qtype:"output",difficulty:3,stem:`A lower one-sided CuSum monitors **mean set-up time** (ms) for a target of 120.

\`\`\`
CUSUM Chart of Set-up Time (ms)
Target = 120   sigma = 6   k = 0.5   h = 5    (K = 3, H = 30)
Lower CUSUM: C-(i) = max[0, (Target - K) - x(i) + C-(i-1)]

Point     x     C- (lower)
  21     116        1
  22     112        6
  23     118        5
  24     109       13
  25     113       17
  26     111       23
  27     111       29
  28     114       32
\`\`\`

Which statement is correct?`,options:["The CuSum signals at point 21, as soon as C⁻ becomes positive","The CuSum signals at point 24, where the lowest single value (109) occurs","The CuSum signals at point 28, where C⁻ first exceeds H = 30; the mean has shifted below target","There is no signal, because every value is inside 120 ± 3σ (102 to 138)"],answer:2,hint:"Signal when the cumulative sum exceeds the decision interval: $C^-_i>H=h\\sigma$.",steps:[{title:"Locate",body:"H = hσ = 5 × 6 = 30. Read the C⁻ column: 29 at point 27, **32** at point 28."},{title:"Check one step",body:"$$C^-_{28}=\\max\\left[0,\\ (120-3)-114+29\\right]=32$$"},{title:"Decide",body:"C⁻ first exceeds 30 at point 28 → the process mean has drifted **below** 120 ms. Each value is small on its own, but the deviations accumulate — exactly what CuSum is for."}],final:"Signal at point 28 (C⁻ = 32 > 30) → answer **C**",whyWrong:["A positive C⁻ is normal accumulation; the signal needs C⁻ > H.","CuSum responds to the accumulated sum, not to the single lowest value; C⁻ is only 13 at point 24.","","That is a Shewhart Test 1 check; a CuSum detects small sustained shifts that never cross 3σ limits."],bookRef:"Book pp. 531, 542"},{id:"sv-5-129",phase:5,topic:"5.2.8",qtype:"concept",difficulty:2,stem:"The book says CuSum is used for the same reasons as EWMA, except that “past data are as important as present data”. What does this mean?",options:["CuSum ignores all data older than the last subgroup","CuSum gives every past deviation from target the same weight in the running sum, while EWMA gives older data geometrically less weight","CuSum and EWMA weight past data identically","CuSum uses only the most recent 8 points, like Test 8"],answer:1,hint:"CuSum: Σ(xᵢ − target) with equal weights; EWMA: weights λ(1 − λ)ʲ that shrink with age.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"A CuSum adds up every deviation from target, so an old deviation counts as much as today’s. EWMA multiplies older observations by (1 − λ) each period, so their influence fades (book p. 531)."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"A Shewhart chart is the one that uses only the latest point; CuSum and EWMA differ precisely in how they weight history."}],final:"Equal weight to all past deviations (EWMA fades them) → answer **B**",whyWrong:["That describes a Shewhart chart, which looks only at the current point.","","They differ: EWMA weights decay with age, CuSum’s do not.","Test 8 is a Shewhart run rule; CuSum accumulates the whole history."],bookRef:"Book p. 531"},{id:"sv-5-130",phase:5,topic:"5.2.8",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:2,stem:"A contact centre’s Xbar chart (n = 5) of **average handle time** has missed a sustained increase of about **0.5σ** that began six weeks ago. Management wants a chart that would catch a shift of this size sooner. Which change is BEST?",options:["Switch to a CuSum chart of the subgroup means","Widen the Xbar limits to ±4σ","Switch to an I-MR chart of individual calls","Switch to an np chart of calls over target"],answer:0,hint:"Small sustained shifts → CuSum (or EWMA); Shewhart charts are best for large, sudden shifts.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book lists CuSum (and EWMA) as special-case charts for detecting **small** shifts that Shewhart charts react to slowly (pp. 530–531)."},{title:"Apply",body:"A 0.5σ shift rarely pushes a single point past 3σ limits, but it steadily builds up the cumulative sum, so CuSum signals much sooner."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Wider limits make detection slower; an I-MR chart (n = 1) is less sensitive than Xbar; an np chart throws away the continuous data."}],final:"CuSum chart → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Wider limits reduce sensitivity further.","Individuals charts are less sensitive than subgroup means, not more.","Converting continuous data to pass/fail loses information and sensitivity."],bookRef:"Book pp. 530–531"},{id:"sv-5-131",phase:5,topic:"5.2.9",qtype:"output",difficulty:3,stem:`\`\`\`
EWMA Chart of Daily Repeat-Fault Rate (%)
Lambda = 0.2   Target = 4.00   Sigma = 0.75

Point     UCL      LCL
   1     4.45     3.55
   2     4.58     3.42
   3     4.64     3.36
  20     4.75     3.25
\`\`\`

Why are the limits **narrower** for the first few points?`,options:["The early EWMA values average fewer observations, so their variance is smaller; the limits widen toward a steady state as more points enter the weighted average","Minitab tightens early limits to compensate for non-normal data","The first points were collected with larger subgroups","The process was more stable at the start of the period"],answer:0,hint:"$$UCL_t=T+3\\sigma\\sqrt{\\dfrac{\\lambda}{2-\\lambda}\\left[1-(1-\\lambda)^{2t}\\right]}$$",steps:[{title:"Locate",body:"Limits widen from ±0.45 at point 1 to ±0.75 at point 20."},{title:"Apply the formula",body:"At t = 1: $$3\\times 0.75\\sqrt{\\dfrac{0.2}{1.8}\\left[1-0.8^{2}\\right]}=0.45$$ As t grows, $(1-\\lambda)^{2t}\\rightarrow 0$ and the half-width approaches $$3\\times 0.75\\sqrt{\\dfrac{0.2}{1.8}}=0.75$$"},{title:"Decide",body:"The width depends only on λ, σ and t — not on the data — because the early EWMA values carry less accumulated variation."}],final:"Early EWMA variance is smaller; limits widen to a steady state → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","The limit width depends only on λ, σ and t, not on any normality adjustment.","The stem uses one sigma and λ for all points; subgroup size is not changing.","Limit width is set by the formula, not by how the data behaved."],bookRef:"Book p. 541"},{id:"sv-5-132",phase:5,topic:"5.2.9",qtype:"concept",difficulty:2,stem:"Why is a **small λ** (for example 0.05–0.2) usually chosen for an EWMA chart?",options:["A small λ makes the chart react most strongly to the latest observation","A small λ turns the EWMA into a Shewhart individuals chart","A small λ gives more weight to past data, smoothing out noise so that small sustained shifts are detected","A small λ removes the need for control limits"],answer:2,hint:"$$z_t=\\lambda x_t+(1-\\lambda)z_{t-1}$$ — small λ → long memory.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"With $z_t=\\lambda x_t+(1-\\lambda)z_{t-1}$, a small λ puts most weight on the running average of history, so random noise is smoothed and a small persistent shift gradually moves z beyond its limits."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"λ = 1 (not small) makes EWMA identical to an individuals chart; EWMA still uses control limits."}],final:"More weight on history → small shifts detected → answer **C**",whyWrong:["A large λ (near 1) reacts most to the latest observation.","Only λ = 1 reproduces an individuals chart.","","EWMA charts use control limits that depend on λ."],bookRef:"Book p. 541"},{id:"sv-5-133",phase:5,topic:"5.2.9",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:1,stem:"A product team records the **daily average app-store rating** (one value per day). Day-to-day values are noisy, but the team wants to spot a small, gradual decline early, giving recent days more weight while still remembering earlier ones. Which chart fits BEST?",options:["np chart","Xbar-R chart","c chart","EWMA chart"],answer:3,hint:"Recent data weighted more, older data weighted less, small shifts → EWMA.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The EWMA statistic $z_t=\\lambda x_t+(1-\\lambda)z_{t-1}$ weights each new value by λ and older values by geometrically smaller amounts — exactly “more weight on recent days, but remember history”."},{title:"Apply",body:"The book lists EWMA for detecting small shifts in noisy or autocorrelated continuous data (p. 531)."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"np and c charts are for attribute counts; Xbar-R needs subgroups and reacts mainly to larger shifts."}],final:"EWMA chart → answer **D**",whyWrong:["A rating is continuous, not a count of defectives.","There is one value per day (no subgroups), and Xbar-R reacts slowly to small shifts.","A c chart is for counts of defects.",""],bookRef:"Book pp. 531, 541"},{id:"sv-5-134",phase:5,topic:"5.2.10",qtype:"calculation",difficulty:2,stem:`An I chart of **ticket backlog** (hundreds) has CL = **50** and UCL = **56**. The last five points are:

**51.0, 50.6, 54.5, 52.1, 55.0**

Which special-cause test fails at the last point?`,options:["Test 1 — one point beyond 3σ","Test 6 — four out of five points beyond 1σ on the same side","Test 5 — two out of three points beyond 2σ on the same side","No test fails"],answer:2,hint:"$$\\sigma=\\dfrac{UCL-CL}{3}$$ then Zone A = beyond 2σ, Zone B = 1σ–2σ, Zone C = within 1σ.",steps:[{title:"Find sigma",body:"$$\\sigma=\\dfrac{56-50}{3}=2$$ so 1σ line = 52, 2σ line = 54, 3σ = 56."},{title:"Check Test 1",body:"No point is above 56."},{title:"Check Test 5",body:"Last three points: 54.5 (beyond 54), 52.1, 55.0 (beyond 54) → **2 of 3 beyond 2σ** on the upper side."},{title:"Check Test 6 and decide",body:"Beyond 1σ (52) in the last five: 54.5, 52.1, 55.0 = 3 of 5 → Test 6 does not fail. **Test 5** fails at the last point."}],final:"Test 5 at the last point → answer **C**",whyWrong:["55.0 is below the UCL of 56.","Only 3 of the last 5 points are beyond 52; Test 6 needs 4.","","Two of the last three points are beyond the 2σ line, which is a Test 5 signal."],bookRef:"Book pp. 534–537"},{id:"sv-5-135",phase:5,topic:"5.2.10",qtype:"calculation",difficulty:3,stem:`An Xbar chart of **order-provisioning time** (min) has CL = **100** and UCL = **112**. Earlier points sat on both sides of the centre line. The last five subgroup means are:

**105.2, 103.1, 106.0, 104.8, 107.3**

Which test fails at the last point?`,options:["Test 2 — nine points in a row on one side","Test 5 — two out of three points beyond 2σ","Test 6 — four out of five points beyond 1σ on the same side","No test fails, because every point is inside the control limits"],answer:2,hint:"$$\\sigma_{\\bar{x}}=\\dfrac{UCL-CL}{3}$$ — then count points beyond the 1σ and 2σ lines.",steps:[{title:"Find sigma",body:"$$\\sigma_{\\bar{x}}=\\dfrac{112-100}{3}=4$$ so 1σ line = 104, 2σ line = 108."},{title:"Count beyond 1σ",body:"105.2 ✓, 103.1 ✗, 106.0 ✓, 104.8 ✓, 107.3 ✓ → **4 of 5** above 104."},{title:"Check the others",body:"None is above 108, so Test 5 does not fail; only five points are above the CL, so Test 2 does not fail."},{title:"Decide",body:"**Test 6** fails — a small upward shift in the mean."}],final:"Test 6 (4 of 5 beyond 1σ) → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Only five consecutive points are above the CL; Test 2 needs nine.","No point is beyond the 2σ line at 108.","","Run tests detect non-random patterns inside the limits; Test 6 fails here."],bookRef:"Book pp. 534–537"},{id:"sv-5-136",phase:5,topic:"5.2.10",qtype:"output",difficulty:2,stem:`\`\`\`
I Chart of Daily Dispatch Delay (min)

TEST 4. 14 points in a row alternating up and down.
Test Failed at points:  27
\`\`\`

The dispatch desk is staffed on alternating days by two teams. What is the MOST likely explanation?`,options:["A gradual upward trend in dispatch delay","A sudden one-off event on day 27","A reduction in variation that should be standardised","A systematic difference between the two alternating teams (or over-adjustment), creating a saw-tooth pattern"],answer:3,hint:"Test 4 (14 alternating) → two alternating sources or tampering.",steps:[{title:"Locate",body:"Test 4 at point 27: points 14–27 alternate up and down."},{title:"Apply",body:"A saw-tooth pattern is non-random. With two teams on alternating days, the obvious cause is that one team is systematically faster than the other. Over-adjustment (reacting to every point) produces the same pattern."},{title:"Decide",body:"Stratify the data by team (or chart each team separately) and investigate the difference."}],final:"Two alternating sources (teams) or over-adjustment → answer **D**",whyWrong:["A trend is Test 3 (six in a row increasing or decreasing).","A one-off event is Test 1; Test 4 describes a 14-point pattern.","Reduced variation is Test 7 (15 points within 1σ).",""],bookRef:"Book pp. 534–537"},{id:"sv-5-137",phase:5,topic:"5.2.10",qtype:"output",difficulty:3,stem:`\`\`\`
Xbar Chart of Install Duration (h)

TEST 8. 8 points in a row more than 1 standard deviation from center line
        (above and below CL).
Test Failed at points:  19
\`\`\`

The subgroups are formed from jobs done by **two contractors**, one consistently quicker than the other, taken on alternate days. What does this result MOST likely indicate?`,options:["A mixture of two populations: points avoid the centre zone because they come from two different contractor means","Unusually low variation that should be kept","A gradual drift in the mean","A single large special cause on day 19"],answer:0,hint:"Test 8: eight points in a row outside Zone C on either side → mixture of two distributions.",steps:[{title:"Locate",body:"Test 8 at point 19: points 12–19 are all more than 1σ from the CL, on both sides."},{title:"Apply",body:"Points that avoid Zone C while landing above and below the CL suggest two separate means mixed together. The book links Test 8 to a mixture or a severe increase in variation."},{title:"Decide",body:"Stratify by contractor and chart each separately."}],final:"Mixture of two populations → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Low variation shows as Test 7 (15 points within Zone C), the opposite pattern.","A drift is Test 3 (six in a row trending).","A single large special cause is Test 1."],bookRef:"Book pp. 534–537"},{id:"sv-5-138",phase:5,topic:"5.2.10",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:1,stem:"An agent on a billing-correction line adjusts the batch settings **every time** the latest point on the I chart is above the centre line, even when no test has failed. Variation has increased. What is happening?",options:["The agent is tampering: reacting to common-cause variation adds variation","The process has a special cause that the chart cannot detect","The agent is following a good response plan","The control limits are too wide and should be replaced by the specification limits"],answer:0,hint:"React only to signals; adjusting on common-cause noise (tampering) increases variation.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book warns that adjusting on every point increases variability; respond only to out-of-control signals with the OCAP (p. 544)."},{title:"Apply",body:"A point above the CL with no test failure is common-cause noise. Each adjustment pushes the process off target in a new direction, so variation grows."},{title:"Decide",body:"Stop adjusting on common-cause points; act only when a test fails."}],final:"Tampering → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","The increase in variation is caused by the adjustments themselves.","A response plan acts on signals, not on every point above the CL.","Control limits must not be replaced by specification limits."],bookRef:"Book p. 544"},{id:"sv-5-139",phase:5,topic:"5.2.10",qtype:"table",difficulty:2,stem:`An I chart of **daily repair time** (h) has CL = **44** and σ = **2** (UCL = 50, LCL = 38).

| Day | 1 | 2 | 3 | 4 | 5 | 6 | 7 | 8 | 9 |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| Hours | 41 | 40 | 42 | 43 | 44.5 | 45 | 46.2 | 47 | 46 |

Which test fails, and at which day?`,options:["Test 1 at day 8","Test 2 at day 9","Test 3 at day 8","No test fails"],answer:2,hint:"Test 3: six points in a row all increasing (six rises, so seven plotted points).",steps:[{title:"Look for a trend",body:"From day 2 (40) every value rises: 42, 43, 44.5, 45, 46.2, 47 — **six consecutive increases** ending at day 8."},{title:"Check the others",body:"Test 1: nothing above 50. Test 2: only days 5–9 (five points) are above 44. Test 5: nothing beyond 48."},{title:"Decide",body:"**Test 3** fails at day 8 — a trend or gradual drift in repair time."}],final:"Test 3 at day 8 → answer **C**",whyWrong:["The highest value (47) is below the UCL of 50.","Only five consecutive days (5–9) are above the CL; Test 2 needs nine.","","Run tests detect patterns inside the limits; the steady rise is a Test 3 signal."],bookRef:"Book pp. 534–537"},{id:"sv-5-140",phase:5,topic:"5.2.10",qtype:"not-except",difficulty:2,stem:"The book notes that some special-cause tests apply to attribute data and some only to variables data. Which test should NOT be applied to a **p chart**?",options:["Test 1 — one point beyond 3σ","Test 2 — nine points in a row on one side of the CL","Test 3 — six points in a row increasing or decreasing","Test 6 — four out of five points beyond 1σ on the same side"],answer:3,hint:"Tests 1–4 apply to attribute charts (and to R, MR, S charts); Tests 5–8 are for variables data.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book states that four tests apply to both variables and attribute data and are the only ones used for R, MR and S charts (p. 532). These are Tests 1–4."},{title:"Apply",body:"Test 6 relies on the zone structure of a symmetric, roughly normal statistic — it is used on Xbar and I charts only."}],final:"Test 6 is NOT applied to a p chart → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Test 1 applies to every Shewhart chart, attribute or variables.","Test 2 is one of the four tests that apply to attribute charts.","Test 3 is one of the four tests that apply to attribute charts.",""],bookRef:"Book p. 532"},{id:"sv-5-141",phase:5,topic:"5.2.10",qtype:"sequence",difficulty:1,stem:`Moving **outward from the centre line** on one side of a control chart, which order of zones is correct?

- **P** — Zone A
- **Q** — Zone B
- **R** — Zone C
- **S** — Beyond the control limit`,options:["P → Q → R → S","R → Q → P → S","Q → R → P → S","R → P → Q → S"],answer:1,hint:"Zone C = within 1σ; Zone B = 1σ–2σ; Zone A = 2σ–3σ; then beyond 3σ.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Each side of the CL is split into three equal zones of width σ: Zone C nearest the CL, then B, then A next to the control limit."},{title:"Order",body:"R (C) → Q (B) → P (A) → S (beyond the limit, a Test 1 signal)."}],final:"C → B → A → beyond → answer **B**",whyWrong:["This reverses the zones: Zone A is next to the control limit, not the centre line.","","Zone C, not Zone B, sits next to the centre line.","Zone B lies between C and A."],bookRef:"Book pp. 534–537"},{id:"sv-5-142",phase:5,topic:"5.3.1",qtype:"calculation",difficulty:2,stem:"An Improvement Selection Matrix uses three outputs with significance **10, 7 and 5**. A candidate control, **automated ticket categorisation**, has impact ratings **6, 4 and 0**, a cost rating of **5** and a time rating of **6**. What is its **overall rating**?",options:["88","528","2,640","6,600"],answer:2,hint:"$$\\text{Overall}=\\Big(\\sum \\text{Sig}_j\\times\\text{Impact}_j\\Big)\\times\\text{Cost}\\times\\text{Time}$$",steps:[{title:"Write what is given",body:"Significance 10, 7, 5; impacts 6, 4, 0; cost 5; time 6."},{title:"Overall impact",body:"$$\\sum \\text{Sig}_j\\times\\text{Impact}_j=10\\times 6+7\\times 4+5\\times 0=60+28+0=88$$"},{title:"Overall rating",body:"$$\\text{Overall}=88\\times 5\\times 6=2640$$"},{title:"Decide",body:"Overall rating = **2,640**. The zero impact on the third output simply adds nothing; only a zero cost or time rating eliminates an option."}],final:"Overall rating = 2,640 → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Stopped at the overall impact (88) and did not multiply by cost and time.","Multiplied by the time rating only (88 × 6), forgetting the cost rating.","","Multiplied the summed significances by the summed impacts (22 × 10 × 30) instead of pairing each output’s significance with its impact."],bookRef:"Book pp. 559–561"},{id:"sv-5-143",phase:5,topic:"5.3.1",qtype:"calculation",difficulty:3,stem:`Outputs and significance: first-time-right **9**, cycle time **6**, complaints **3**. Four candidate controls are rated (impact 0–7 per output; cost and time 0–7):

| Option | Impact FTR | Impact cycle | Impact complaints | Cost | Time |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| P — New validation engine | 7 | 2 | 1 | 3 | 5 |
| Q — Mandatory-field checks | 4 | 4 | 4 | 5 | 4 |
| R — Auto-routing rules | 5 | 5 | 0 | 6 | 3 |
| S — Visual queue board | 2 | 3 | 6 | 6 | 6 |

Which option ranks **SECOND**?`,options:["Option P","Option Q","Option R","Option S"],answer:1,hint:"$$\\text{Overall}=\\Big(\\sum \\text{Sig}_j\\times\\text{Impact}_j\\Big)\\times\\text{Cost}\\times\\text{Time}$$",steps:[{title:"Overall impact",body:`$$P=9\\times 7+6\\times 2+3\\times 1=78$$

$$Q=9\\times 4+6\\times 4+3\\times 4=72$$

$$R=9\\times 5+6\\times 5+3\\times 0=75$$

$$S=9\\times 2+6\\times 3+3\\times 6=54$$`},{title:"Overall rating",body:`$$P=78\\times 3\\times 5=1170$$

$$Q=72\\times 5\\times 4=1440$$

$$R=75\\times 6\\times 3=1350$$

$$S=54\\times 6\\times 6=1944$$`},{title:"Decide",body:"Ranking: S (1,944) → **Q (1,440)** → R (1,350) → P (1,170). The highest-impact option (P) comes last because it is expensive and slow."}],final:"Q ranks second (1,440) → answer **B**",whyWrong:["P has the highest overall impact (78) but ranks last (1,170) once cost and time are applied.","","R ranks third (1,350).","S ranks first (1,944), not second."],bookRef:"Book pp. 559–561"},{id:"sv-5-144",phase:5,topic:"5.3.1",qtype:"table",difficulty:2,stem:`Two controls are proposed for SIM-swap fraud checks (OMR thousands, no discounting):

| Option | One-time cost | Running cost per year | Saving per year |
|---|---|---|---|
| A — Automated identity check | 30 | 5 | 25 |
| B — Extra manual reviewer | 5 | 18 | 28 |

Over a **3-year** life, which statement is correct?`,options:["A gives the higher net benefit, 45, because its saving is 75 against a one-time cost of 30","B is better because it pays back faster (about 0.5 years against 1.5 years)","A gives the higher net benefit: 30 against 25 for B","B gives the higher net benefit because its one-time cost is much lower"],answer:2,hint:"Net benefit = total saving − one-time cost − (running cost × years).",steps:[{title:"Option A",body:"$$\\text{Net}_A=3\\times 25-30-3\\times 5=75-30-15=30$$"},{title:"Option B",body:"$$\\text{Net}_B=3\\times 28-5-3\\times 18=84-5-54=25$$"},{title:"Payback (screen only)",body:"$$\\text{Payback}_A=\\dfrac{30}{25-5}=1.5\\text{ yr}\\qquad \\text{Payback}_B=\\dfrac{5}{28-18}=0.5\\text{ yr}$$"},{title:"Decide",body:"A creates more value over the control’s life (30 vs 25) even though B pays back faster. Payback is a screen; total value decides."}],final:"A: net 30 vs B: net 25 → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Forgot the running cost: 75 − 30 = 45 overstates A.","Payback ignores everything after the crossover; over 3 years B creates less value.","","A low one-time cost does not offset B’s high running cost (54 over 3 years)."],trap:"Recurring costs of a control are paid every year — include them for the whole life.",bookRef:"Book pp. 557–558"},{id:"sv-5-145",phase:5,topic:"5.3.1",qtype:"concept",difficulty:2,stem:"In the book’s Improvement Selection Matrix, where should the **significance ratings** of the outputs come from?",options:["From the updated X-Y Matrix rankings of the project’s outputs","From the cost of each candidate improvement","From the time each improvement will take to implement","From the RPNs of the original FMEA"],answer:0,hint:"Significance (1–10) per output must match the updated X-Y Matrix.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book adapts a familiar tool: list the outputs (Y’s) across the top and give each a significance rating from 1 to 10 that **matches the updated X-Y Matrix rankings** (pp. 559–560)."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Cost and time are separate 0–7 ratings that multiply the impact; FMEA RPNs rank failure modes, not outputs."}],final:"Updated X-Y Matrix rankings → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Cost is its own 0–7 rating applied after the impact is summed.","Time is its own 0–7 rating applied after the impact is summed.","RPNs rank failure modes and causes, not the importance of each output."],bookRef:"Book pp. 559–560"},{id:"sv-5-146",phase:5,topic:"5.3.1",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:2,stem:"The top-rated improvement needs a **7-month** capital procurement for new test equipment. Four other approved improvements can be in place within weeks. What does the book recommend?",options:["Wait until the equipment arrives so that all improvements go live together","Drop the top-rated improvement because its time rating is low","Implement only the top-rated improvement and cancel the others","Implement the quick improvements now and run the long-lead purchase in parallel, managed with a timeline or Gantt chart"],answer:3,hint:"Long-lead items run in parallel so they do not delay everything else.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book cautions teams not to forget to run long-lead items such as capital purchases **in parallel**, managed with a timeline or Gantt chart (p. 561)."},{title:"Apply",body:"The quick wins deliver benefit now; the procurement proceeds alongside and is tracked to completion."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Waiting delays all benefits by seven months; a low (but non-zero) time rating has already been reflected in the score; cancelling approved quick wins wastes value."}],final:"Quick wins now; long-lead item in parallel → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Holding back ready improvements delays benefits for no reason.","Its time rating is already in the score — it was still ranked top.","The other improvements were approved on their own merits; cancelling them loses value.",""],bookRef:"Book p. 561"},{id:"sv-5-147",phase:5,topic:"5.3.1",qtype:"true-false",difficulty:2,stem:"Order volume grew 15% during the project. To confirm realised savings, the team should compare the **raw count** of errors before and after, without adjusting for the change in volume. True or False?",options:["True","False"],answer:1,hint:"Normalise for volume and mix: compare rates, not raw counts.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"When volume changes, raw counts mix the improvement with growth. Comparing **rates** (errors per order) isolates the effect of the change before benefits are signed off with Finance."},{title:"Apply",body:"With 15% more orders, an unchanged error rate would show 15% more errors — hiding part of a real saving."}],final:"False — compare rates → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Raw counts confound the improvement with the 15% volume growth.",""],bookRef:"Book pp. 577–578"},{id:"sv-5-148",phase:5,topic:"5.3.2",qtype:"not-except",difficulty:1,stem:"The book lists five elements of a control plan. All of the following are elements EXCEPT:",options:["Monitoring plan","Response plan","Design of experiments plan","Aligning systems and structures"],answer:2,hint:"Five elements: documentation, monitoring, response, training, aligning systems & structures.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Book p. 564: a control plan has five elements — **documentation, monitoring, response, training, and aligning systems and structures**."},{title:"Apply",body:"A design of experiments belongs to Improve, where the solution is found; it is not part of sustaining it."}],final:"DOE plan is NOT a control-plan element → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Monitoring is one of the five elements.","Response is one of the five elements.","","Aligning systems and structures is one of the five elements."],bookRef:"Book p. 564"},{id:"sv-5-149",phase:5,topic:"5.3.2",qtype:"concept",difficulty:1,stem:"The book calls the control plan a **living document**. What does this mean in practice?",options:["It is written once at project closure and then archived","It is updated whenever the process, its limits, its FMEA or its owners change","It can only be changed by the Green Belt who wrote it","It replaces the standard operating procedures"],answer:1,hint:"Living = kept current by the process owner as the process changes.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book describes the control plan as a written summary and a living document (p. 562): as the process, control limits, FMEA risks or responsibilities change, the plan is revised."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Archiving it defeats its purpose; the process owner — not the departing Belt — maintains it; it summarises and references SOPs rather than replacing them."}],final:"Kept current as the process changes → answer **B**",whyWrong:["A plan that is archived is no longer used to run the process.","","The process owner maintains the plan after the Belt moves on.","The book states explicitly that a control plan is not a replacement for SOPs."],bookRef:"Book p. 562"},{id:"sv-5-150",phase:5,topic:"5.3.2",qtype:"concept",difficulty:2,stem:"What is meant by the control-plan element **“aligning systems and structures”**?",options:["Adjusting performance goals, policies, job descriptions and incentives so that they support the new way of working","Aligning the control limits with the specification limits","Making sure the measurement system passes a Gage R&R study","Aligning the project timeline with the finance calendar"],answer:0,hint:"Systems and structures are what make the behaviour change permanent.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book says systems and structures are the basis for allowing people to change their behaviour permanently: performance goals, policies and procedures, job descriptions, incentive compensation and programmes (pp. 572–573)."},{title:"Apply",body:"If an hourly incentive still rewards speed over accuracy, staff will drift back to the old way regardless of SOPs and charts. Removing measures that are not aligned with the desired behaviour is part of this element."}],final:"Goals, policies, roles and incentives support the change → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Control limits are never aligned with specifications.","MSA is part of the monitoring plan, not this element.","Timeline alignment is project management, not a control-plan element."],bookRef:"Book pp. 572–573"},{id:"sv-5-151",phase:5,topic:"5.3.2",qtype:"concept",difficulty:2,stem:"Why does the book state that a control plan is **not a replacement for SOPs**?",options:["Because SOPs are only needed in manufacturing","Because the control plan is a summary of what is controlled and how, and it references the SOPs that describe how the work itself is done","Because SOPs are more effective controls than poka-yoke","Because the control plan is only used during the project"],answer:1,hint:"Control plan = summary of controls; SOPs = detailed work instructions.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The control plan is a written summary: what is measured, how, how often, by whom and what happens on a signal (p. 562). The detailed method lives in SOPs, which the documentation plan keeps current."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"SOPs apply to service processes too; the book ranks SOPs as the weakest control; the control plan is used long after the project closes."}],final:"Summary that references SOPs → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Service processes need SOPs just as much as manufacturing does.","","The book ranks an SOP as the least effective control, below poka-yoke.","The control plan is handed to the process owner and used after closure."],bookRef:"Book p. 562"},{id:"sv-5-152",phase:5,topic:"5.3.2",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:1,stem:"Six months after handoff, error rates in a provisioning team start to climb. The new joiners hired since handoff were given the SOP link but no structured instruction, and no refresher exists for existing staff. Which control-plan element is MOST clearly missing?",options:["A training plan covering initial and ongoing training, triggered also by the response plan when performance degrades","A larger subgroup size on the control chart","A new DMAIC project","A higher specification limit"],answer:0,hint:"Training is one of the five control-plan elements; the book ties ongoing training to the response plan.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The training plan defines who is trained, on what and when — including new joiners and refreshers. The book notes that ongoing training is triggered as part of the response plan when monitoring shows performance degrading (p. 565)."},{title:"Apply",body:"New staff with only a link to the SOP is the classic gap: the knowledge left with the original team."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Chart settings and specs do not teach anyone; a new project is premature when the cause is a known control gap."}],final:"Training plan → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Chart sensitivity does not fix the missing knowledge.","The cause is a known gap in the control plan, not an unknown problem needing a new project.","Moving the specification hides the errors instead of preventing them."],bookRef:"Book pp. 565–566"},{id:"sv-5-153",phase:5,topic:"5.3.2",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:3,stem:"A critical X — the customer’s port-in PIN — is now validated by a **Type 1 prevention poka-yoke** that blocks any request with an invalid PIN. The draft control plan still requires an **hourly** SPC chart on PIN errors. What is the BEST revision?",options:["Replace the hourly chart with a periodic check that the poka-yoke is working (for example a daily test with a known-bad PIN), with an owner and a reaction if it fails","Remove all monitoring, because a poka-yoke cannot fail","Keep the hourly chart and add 100% manual inspection as well","Switch the hourly chart to a CuSum chart"],answer:0,hint:"Match the monitoring effort to the strength of the control; verify the device, not the defect.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"A Type 1 control makes the error impossible while it works, so the residual risk is the device failing. The book lists automation failure as a response-plan trigger (p. 571)."},{title:"Apply",body:"An hourly chart of an error that cannot occur adds cost and no information. A scheduled functional check of the device — with an owner and a reaction — controls the real risk."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Adding inspection duplicates a stronger control; removing all checks ignores device failure; a CuSum still monitors the wrong thing."}],final:"Periodic verification of the poka-yoke → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Devices and software rules can fail; they need periodic verification.","Inspection on top of a prevention device adds cost without reducing risk.","Changing the chart type still monitors errors that the device prevents."],bookRef:"Book pp. 522, 568–571"},{id:"sv-5-154",phase:5,topic:"5.3.2",qtype:"table",difficulty:3,stem:`Three rows of a draft control plan for a fibre-repair process are shown:

| Row | Characteristic | Limits used | Method and frequency | Owner | Reaction |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| 1 | Repair time (h) | Customer SLA of 4 h drawn as the UCL | I-MR, daily | Field ops lead | Follow OCAP-07 |
| 2 | Repeat-fault rate | p chart limits from 25 weeks | p chart, weekly | — | Follow OCAP-08 |
| 3 | Splice loss (dB) | Xbar-R limits from 25 subgroups | Xbar-R (n = 4), each shift | Splicing supervisor | Follow OCAP-09 |

Which row is complete and correct?`,options:["Row 1","Row 2","Row 3","None of the rows"],answer:2,hint:"Each row needs process-based limits, a method and frequency, a named owner and a reaction.",steps:[{title:"Check row 1",body:"The SLA (a specification) is drawn as the UCL — control limits must come from process data."},{title:"Check row 2",body:"No owner — nobody is accountable for acting on a signal."},{title:"Check row 3 and decide",body:"Process-based limits, a rational subgroup and frequency, a named owner and a defined OCAP: **row 3** is complete."}],final:"Row 3 → answer **C**",whyWrong:["A specification (the 4-hour SLA) has been used as a control limit.","There is no owner, so no one is accountable for reacting.","","Row 3 has every required element."],bookRef:"Book pp. 562–570"},{id:"sv-5-155",phase:5,topic:"5.3.2",qtype:"sequence",difficulty:2,stem:`In the book’s Control roadmap, after the improvement is selected, which order of “develop and implement” steps is correct?

- **P** — Monitoring plan
- **Q** — Training plan
- **R** — Response plan
- **S** — Documentation plan`,options:["Q → S → P → R","S → Q → R → P","P → R → Q → S","Q → P → S → R"],answer:0,hint:"Roadmap: Training → Documentation → Monitoring → Response → Align systems & structures → Verify financial impact.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book’s Control roadmap runs: develop and implement the **training** plan, then the **documentation** plan, then the **monitoring** plan, then the **response** plan, then align systems and structures, and finally verify the financial impact."},{title:"Order",body:"Q (training) → S (documentation) → P (monitoring) → R (response). The response plan comes after monitoring because it reacts to what monitoring detects."}],final:"Training → Documentation → Monitoring → Response → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","The roadmap starts with training, and the response plan follows monitoring.","Monitoring and response come after training and documentation.","Documentation comes before monitoring in the roadmap."],bookRef:"Book pp. 577–578"},{id:"sv-5-156",phase:5,topic:"5.3.3",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:1,stem:"At 01:00 the automated address-validation rule that prevents failed installations goes **offline**. No control chart has signalled yet because no failed installations have been recorded. Should the response plan be triggered?",options:["No — the response plan is triggered only by an out-of-control point on a chart","No — wait until the p chart shows Test 1 before acting","Yes, but only after the next weekly review meeting","Yes — automation failure is one of the book’s response-plan triggers, so the defined response starts now"],answer:3,hint:"Book triggers: out-of-control point, non-random pattern, defect-producing condition present, check-sheet failure, automation failure.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book lists five triggers for the response plan: an out-of-control point; non-random behaviour within the limits; a condition proven to produce defects being present; a check-sheet failure; and an **automation failure** (p. 571)."},{title:"Apply",body:"The control itself has failed. Waiting for the chart means waiting for customers to be hit; the response (manual validation, restore the rule) should start immediately."}],final:"Yes — automation failure is a trigger → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Chart signals are only one of five triggers.","Waiting for the chart lets defects reach customers first.","A weekly meeting is far too slow; the trigger calls for the defined response now.",""],bookRef:"Book p. 571"},{id:"sv-5-157",phase:5,topic:"5.3.3",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:3,stem:"Each time the p chart of failed number ports signals, the on-call engineer fixes the immediate problem but writes nothing down. Three months later nobody can say how often each cause occurred, and the same causes keep returning. What should be added to the response plan?",options:["An abnormality report that records the situation, the cause found, the corrective action and the root-cause analysis for every signal","Tighter control limits so that signals appear earlier","A rule that only the Green Belt may respond to signals","Removal of the p chart, since signals are being fixed anyway"],answer:0,hint:"Response-plan documentation: record each failure mode, cause and action so that patterns can be fixed permanently.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book’s response-plan components include documentation of the response and detailed information on the conditions, captured in an **abnormality report**: situation, cause, corrective action and root-cause analysis — like an ISO 9000 corrective-action request (p. 572)."},{title:"Apply",body:"Without records, recurring causes are invisible. The reports give the frequency of each cause and feed permanent prevention and FMEA updates."}],final:"Abnormality report for every signal → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Tighter limits create false alarms and do nothing about recurring causes.","The process owner’s team must respond; the Belt has moved on.","Removing the chart removes the detection, not the causes."],bookRef:"Book p. 572"},{id:"sv-5-158",phase:5,topic:"5.3.3",qtype:"concept",difficulty:1,stem:"Which of the following is one of the book’s **components of a response plan**?",options:["The responsibilities for responding to each failure mode","The Gage R&R study for each measurement","The project charter and business case","The hypothesis tests used in Analyze"],answer:0,hint:"Components: triggers, recommended response, responsibilities, documentation of the response, conditions surrounding the failure.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Book p. 571: the response plan’s components are the **triggers** (which failure modes to check, usually the highest-risk X’s), the **recommended response**, the **responsibilities** for responding, **documentation** that the plan was followed, and **detailed information** on the conditions surrounding the failure mode."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"The charter, MSA and hypothesis tests are earlier-phase deliverables."}],final:"Responsibilities for responding → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","MSA belongs to Measure (and to the monitoring plan), not to the response plan’s components.","The charter belongs to Define.","Hypothesis tests belong to Analyze."],bookRef:"Book p. 571"},{id:"sv-5-159",phase:5,topic:"5.3.3",qtype:"not-except",difficulty:2,stem:"All of the following are triggers for the response plan EXCEPT:",options:["A point beyond the control limits","Non-random behaviour within the control limits, such as nine points on one side","A failed item on a daily check sheet","A single point inside the limits that is slightly above the centre line"],answer:3,hint:"Triggers are signals or failures — not ordinary common-cause variation.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book’s triggers: out-of-control point, non-random behaviour within limits, a defect-producing condition present, check-sheet failure, automation failure (p. 571)."},{title:"Apply",body:"A single point slightly above the CL with no test failure is common-cause variation. Reacting to it is tampering."}],final:"A normal point near the CL is NOT a trigger → answer **D**",whyWrong:["A point beyond the limits is the classic trigger.","Non-random patterns inside the limits are a listed trigger.","Check-sheet failure is a listed trigger.",""],bookRef:"Book p. 571"},{id:"sv-5-160",phase:5,topic:"5.3.3",qtype:"sequence",difficulty:2,stem:`An Xbar chart of splice loss signals on the night shift. Following the book’s OCAP guidance, put the steps in order:

- **P** — Wait for the next subgroup and plot it
- **Q** — Follow the OCAP: check the measurement, find the cause and correct it
- **R** — If the new point is back in control, make no further adjustments and record the actions taken
- **S** — Recognise the out-of-control signal on the chart`,options:["Q → S → P → R","S → P → Q → R","S → Q → R → P","S → Q → P → R"],answer:3,hint:"Signal → OCAP actions → wait for the next sample → if in control, stop adjusting and document.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book’s SPC steps: respond to the signal using the OCAP; after the correction, **wait for the next sample**; if it is back in control, make **no further changes** and record what was done (pp. 547–548)."},{title:"Order",body:"S (signal) → Q (OCAP) → P (next subgroup) → R (no further changes, record)."}],final:"S → Q → P → R → answer **D**",whyWrong:["The OCAP cannot start before the signal is recognised.","Waiting for the next subgroup before acting leaves the problem uncorrected.","You cannot judge “back in control” before plotting the next subgroup.",""],bookRef:"Book pp. 544, 547–548"}];export{e as items};
